-- Lecture-notes removal, part 5 of 9. Paste ALL of this into the Supabase SQL editor and Run.
-- One transaction: if any essay was edited since the snapshot, nothing in this part is written.
BEGIN;

-- intangible-assets-net-software-land-rights-goodwill
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>An intangible asset is an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance. Identifiability, meaning separable or arising from a contract or legal right, is what divides it from goodwill.</p></li><li><p>Bought intangibles are recorded at cost, or at fair value when acquired in a business combination. Internally generated ones face a stricter rule. Research is always expensed, development is capitalised only from the date six criteria are all met, and brands and customer lists built in-house are never recognised.</p></li><li><p>A finite-life intangible is amortised over the shorter of its economic and legal life, normally straight-line with zero residual value. An indefinite-life intangible and goodwill are not amortised but tested for impairment every year, and a goodwill impairment is never reversed.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report intangible assets, net, of 71,814 at 31 December 2024 against 73,800 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 0.24 percent of total assets and 0.32 percent of non-current assets (0.24 and 0.35 percent a year earlier). It holds software and licences, land rights, and goodwill from two acquisitions (Note 13, Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121).</p><p>A student should be able to recognise, measure, amortise and impair an intangible, and compute and test goodwill, with journals.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standards are PSAK 238 <em>Aset Takberwujud</em> (formerly PSAK 19, IAS 38) and, for goodwill, PSAK 103 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis</em> (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3).</p><h2>1. What an intangible asset is</h2><p>PSAK 238 defines an intangible asset as an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance (para. 8). Kieso reduces this to four characteristics: identifiable, no physical form, benefits over more than one period, and not held for sale. Control normally comes from legal rights (para. 13), and benefits include cost savings (para. 17).</p><p><strong>Identifiability</strong> separates intangible assets from goodwill (para. 11). An item is identifiable if it is <em>separable</em>, capable of being sold, transferred or licensed on its own, or if it arises from contractual or other legal rights, transferable or not (para. 12). A software licence is both. A trained workforce is neither, so it stays in goodwill.</p><p>Kieso sorts intangibles into types, which help when an acquisition is broken down (section 7).</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Type</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Examples</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Marketing-related</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trademarks, trade names, domain names</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Customer-related</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Customer lists, order backlogs, customer relationships</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Artistic-related</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Copyrights on books, music, pictures, video</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contract-related</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Franchises, licences, permits, concessions</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Technology-related</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Patents, software, databases, trade secrets</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>The residual in a business combination (not identifiable)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Boundaries.</strong> Software integral to hardware, such as a machine's operating system, is part of the hardware under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16) (para. 4). PSAK 238 excludes exploration and evaluation assets, which follow PSAK 106 (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6), and oil, gas and mineral development and extraction (para. 2), but not software used there (para. 7).</p><h2>2. Acquired intangible assets</h2><p>A separately acquired intangible is measured at cost (para. 24). The recognition tests, probable benefits and reliable cost, are treated as met for a purchase, because the price reflects the buyer's expectation of benefit (paras. 25–26). Cost is the purchase price, including non-refundable taxes, less trade discounts, plus every directly attributable cost of preparing the asset for use, such as professional fees and testing (paras. 27–28). Training staff and general overhead are expensed (para. 29).</p><p>For example (illustration), within PHE's 2024 software additions of 14,038 (Note 13), suppose one item is a perpetual licence for reservoir simulation software, list price 2,000, with a 10 percent volume discount. The vendor charges 250 to install and integrate it, testing before go-live costs 120, training the engineers costs 80, and first-year support is 150, expensed over the support year.</p><blockquote><p>Purchase price = 2,000 × (1 − 10%) = 1,800</p><p>Cost = 1,800 + 250 installation + 120 testing = 2,170</p><p>Kept out of cost = 80 training (expensed) + 150 support (prepaid, expensed over the year) = 230</p><p>Dr Intangible assets, software 2,170, Dr General and administrative expenses 80, Dr Prepaid expenses 150 / Cr Trade payables 2,400</p></blockquote><p>An intangible received in exchange for a non-monetary asset is measured at fair value unless the exchange lacks commercial substance or neither fair value can be measured reliably (paras. 45–46), the rule the Fixed Assets essay works through. One acquired in a business combination is measured at acquisition-date fair value, separately from goodwill (paras. 33–34).</p><h2>3. Internally generated intangibles: research and development</h2><p>No market price confirms the value of an intangible built in-house, so PSAK 238 divides each internal project into two phases to decide when spending starts creating an asset (para. 52).</p><ul><li><p><em>Research</em> is original and planned investigation to gain new scientific or technical knowledge (para. 8), such as searching for and evaluating alternatives (para. 56). No asset is recognised from it, and the spending is expensed when incurred (para. 54).</p></li><li><p><em>Development</em> is applying research findings to a plan or design for new or substantially improved products, processes or systems before commercial use (para. 8), such as designing and testing prototypes (para. 59). It is capitalised only when the entity can show all six criteria (para. 57):</p><ol><li><p>technical feasibility of completing the asset;</p></li><li><p>the intention to complete it and use or sell it;</p></li><li><p>the ability to use or sell it;</p></li><li><p>how it will generate probable future benefits, through a market or, if used internally, its usefulness;</p></li><li><p>adequate technical, financial and other resources to finish it;</p></li><li><p>the ability to measure its cost reliably.</p></li></ol></li></ul><p>If research cannot be told apart from development, the whole project is treated as research (para. 53). Cost starts from the date the criteria are first met, and earlier spending stays in expense and cannot be reinstated (paras. 65, 71). Only directly attributable costs qualify; overhead and training do not (paras. 66–67). Under PSAK 238 personnel and contract-service costs follow the phase, capitalised in development once the criteria are met, and separately purchased items are measured at cost (para. 24). Materials and equipment with alternative future uses become inventory or fixed assets.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary builds production-surveillance software in 2024. From January to March it studies feasibility and evaluates vendor products (300) and codes a rough prototype (150). On 1 April the budget is approved and the design is shown to work, so all six criteria are met. From April to December it spends 700 on programmers, 260 on contract coders and 140 on testing, and is charged 90 of head-office overhead and 60 for training field operators.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Before 1 April (criteria not met)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>From 1 April (criteria met)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expensed</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>research 300 + development before criteria 150 = 450</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>90 overhead + 60 training = 150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Capitalised</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>none</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>700 + 260 + 140 = 1,100</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Dr Research and development expense 450 / Cr Cash and accrued expenses 450</p><p>Dr Intangible assets, software under development 1,100, Dr General and administrative expenses 150 / Cr Cash and accrued expenses 1,250</p></blockquote><p>Of 1,700 spent, 1,100 becomes an asset. The 150 prototype is development work (para. 59), but it came before the criteria were met, so it stays in expense although it fed the final product (para. 71).</p><p><strong>What is never capitalised.</strong> Internally generated brands, mastheads, publishing titles and customer lists are not recognised, because their cost cannot be told apart from developing the business as a whole (paras. 63–64). PSAK 238.63 does not recognise an internally generated brand at all, so the attorney, registration, design, consulting and successful defence costs of a self-developed trademark are expensed.</p><p><strong>Cloud software.</strong> The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in 2019 that a software-as-a-service (SaaS) contract is usually a service, and in 2021 that configuring or customising the supplier's software is expensed as the service is received unless it creates an asset the customer controls. For example (illustration), a three-year SaaS contract for a production-accounting system costs 150 a year, and the supplier charges 90 up front for configuration that is not distinct from the subscription. The 150 is expensed each year, and the 90 is a prepayment expensed at 90 / 3 = 30 a year.</p><p><strong>The upstream parallel.</strong> Geologists' work is an example of research, and PHE likewise expenses geological and geophysical costs under PSAK 106 (Note 3m(i), Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73; see the Oil and Gas Properties essay).</p><h2>4. Amortisation</h2><p>How an intangible is amortised depends on whether its useful life is finite or indefinite (paras. 88–89). An intangible with a finite life is amortised. One with an indefinite life, with no foreseeable limit to the period over which it will bring in net cash, is not (para. 107).</p><p><strong>Useful life</strong> reflects expected use, obsolescence, competitors' actions, maintenance needed and legal limits (para. 90). A right arising from a contract or law is amortised over no more than its term (para. 94). Renewal periods count only if renewal is evidenced, its conditions will be met and its cost is small relative to its benefits (para. 96).</p><p><strong>Residual value</strong> is assumed to be zero unless a third party has committed to buy the asset or an active market will exist at the end of its life (para. 100).</p><p><strong>Method.</strong> Amortisation starts when the asset is available for use and follows the pattern in which benefits are consumed, with straight-line when that pattern cannot be determined reliably (paras. 97–98). A revenue-based method is presumed inappropriate, because revenue reflects prices and volumes as well as use (para. 98A), unless the right itself is defined by a revenue limit (paras. 98B–98C). Period and method are reviewed each year end, and a change is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8) (para. 104).</p><p>For example (illustration), the surveillance software goes live on 1 January 2025 with a five-year life. The simulation licence is perpetual, but the subsidiary expects to replace it after seven years as the technology dates.</p><blockquote><p>Surveillance software = 1,100 / 5 = 220 a year; simulation licence = 2,170 / 7 = 310 a year</p><p>Dr Amortisation expense 530 / Cr Accumulated amortisation, software 530</p></blockquote><p>At the end of 2026 the surveillance software carries 1,100 − 2 × 220 = 660. If its remaining life is revised to two years, amortisation becomes 660 / 2 = 330 a year from 2027, with no restatement.</p><p><strong>Land rights.</strong> Indonesian land rights such as <em>hak guna bangunan</em> (HGB, a right to build) run for a term and can be extended. PHE's policy puts the initial legal costs of a land right into land, undepreciated, and treats rights that are leases in substance under PSAK 116 (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69; the Right-of-Use Assets essay explains the ISAK 336 test). Extension and renewal costs have a term of their own, so they are commonly carried as an intangible and amortised over the extended term. For example (illustration), fees of 60 to extend an HGB for 20 years are amortised at 60 / 20 = 3 a year.</p><p><strong>Indefinite life.</strong> For example (illustration), a subsidiary buys a trade name for 5,000, renewable every ten years at a small fee, with no plan to stop using it. It is not amortised but tested for impairment each year (para. 108), and the life assessment is reviewed each period (para. 109). If in 2027 management decides to retire the name in four years, it is tested for impairment and then amortised at 5,000 / 4 = 1,250 a year.</p><h2>5. Subsequent costs, revaluation and derecognition</h2><p>Later spending is only rarely capitalised, because most of it maintains benefits already expected (para. 20). Under PSAK 238 the cost of successfully defending a patent keeps existing benefits and is normally expensed.</p><p>An entity may choose the revaluation model for a class of intangibles only where fair value can be measured in an <em>active market</em> (paras. 72, 75). Patents, brands and software are unique, so the model is rare. PHE uses cost.</p><p>An intangible is derecognised on disposal or when no further benefit is expected, with the gain or loss in profit or loss. For example (illustration), take PHE's 2024 software deductions (Note 13), cost 5,580 and accumulated amortisation 2,768, as systems retired with no proceeds.</p><blockquote><p>Carrying amount = 5,580 − 2,768 = 2,812</p><p>Dr Accumulated amortisation, software 2,768, Dr Loss on derecognition 2,812 / Cr Intangible assets, software 5,580</p></blockquote><h2>6. Impairment</h2><p>An intangible is written down when its carrying amount exceeds its <em>recoverable amount</em>, the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, under PSAK 236 <em>Penurunan Nilai Aset</em> (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36). The mechanism is taught in the Fixed Assets and Oil and Gas Properties essays; intangibles differ in when the test is required.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Asset</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>When tested</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Reversal</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Finite-life intangible</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>When there is an indication of impairment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allowed, up to the carrying amount with no impairment</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Indefinite-life intangible</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Every year, and on an indication</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allowed, same cap</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not yet available for use</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Every year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allowed, same cap</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Every year, within a cash-generating unit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Never (PSAK 236.124)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The test is against recoverable amount, not an appraised fair value alone. Fair value less costs of disposal is only one leg; value in use may be higher when the asset is worth more in the entity's own use. PHE's policy lists the same annual tests (Note 3w, Daftar 5/72, PDF p. 89).</p><p>For example (illustration), suppose instead that the surveillance software served one field, and in late 2025 the subsidiary's contract for that field is not extended. The software cannot be sold, so fair value less costs of disposal is nil, and value in use for the remaining months is 250.</p><blockquote><p>Carrying amount at 31 December 2025 = 1,100 − 220 = 880</p><p>Impairment loss = 880 − higher of (0, 250) = 630</p><p>Dr Impairment loss on non-financial assets 630 / Cr Allowance for impairment, software 630</p></blockquote><h2>7. Goodwill</h2><p>Goodwill is what a buyer pays beyond the fair value of the identifiable assets and liabilities it gets, such as the assembled workforce and expected synergies. It is not identifiable, so it falls outside PSAK 238 (para. 3(f)) and is measured under PSAK 103. Internally generated goodwill is never recognised.</p><p><strong>Measurement.</strong> Under the acquisition method the acquirer measures identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value at the acquisition date, including intangibles the acquiree never recorded. Goodwill is the residual. Acquisition costs are expensed; PHE puts them in general and administrative expenses (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52).</p><blockquote><p>Goodwill = consideration transferred + non-controlling interest (NCI) − fair value of identifiable net assets</p></blockquote><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 100 percent of an oilfield-services company for 120,000 cash.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Book value</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fair value</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fixed assets</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>48,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>61,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Inventories</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>11,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receivables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Customer contracts (order backlog)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>none</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Software</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Liabilities</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(16,500)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(16,500)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Net assets</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>62,000</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>84,000</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Goodwill = 120,000 − 84,000 = 36,000</p><p>Dr Fixed assets 61,500, Dr Inventories 12,000, Dr Receivables 10,500, Dr Cash 7,500, Dr Intangible assets, customer contracts 6,000, Dr Intangible assets, software 3,000, Dr Goodwill 36,000 / Cr Liabilities 16,500, Cr Cash 120,000</p></blockquote><p>Of the 58,000 paid above book value, 22,000 is fair value step-ups, including 6,000 of customer contracts the seller never recorded, and 36,000 is goodwill. Deferred tax on the step-ups is ignored here; PHE carries a deferred tax liability of 7,760 labelled "excess fair value over NBV" (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172).</p><p><strong>Non-controlling interest.</strong> When less than 100 percent is bought, PSAK 103 lets the acquirer measure NCI, acquisition by acquisition, at fair value or at its proportionate share of identifiable net assets, a choice PHE's policy states (Note 3d). Suppose the subsidiary buys 80 percent for 96,000, and the other 20 percent has a fair value of 23,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI at proportionate share</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI at fair value</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20% × 84,000 = 16,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>96,000 + 16,800 − 84,000 = 28,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>96,000 + 23,000 − 84,000 = 35,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Whose goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>The parent's only</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>The parent's and the NCI's</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Bargain purchase.</strong> A price below the fair value of net assets is first rechecked, and any excess left is a gain in profit or loss. Bought for 80,000, the company above gives a gain of 84,000 − 80,000 = 4,000.</p><p><strong>No amortisation.</strong> PSAK 22 (1994) amortised goodwill over five to twenty years; the revision that adopted IFRS 3 replaced amortisation with an annual impairment test. PHE's Note 13 still shows the old amortisation (section 9).</p><p><strong>Impairment of goodwill.</strong> Goodwill brings in no cash on its own, so from the acquisition date it is allocated to the cash-generating units (CGUs) expected to benefit from the combination (PHE Note 3d, Daftar 5/37, PDF p. 54), and each is tested every year (Note 3w, Daftar 5/73, PDF p. 90). A unit's impairment loss goes first to goodwill and then to its other assets pro rata to carrying amount (PSAK 236.104), with no asset taken below the highest of its fair value less costs of disposal, value in use and zero (para. 105). For example (illustration), a one-block CGU carries oil and gas properties of 400,000, goodwill of 50,000 and current assets of 30,000, and lower price forecasts cut its recoverable amount to 420,000. The current assets (receivables and inventories) are measured under their own standards and sit outside PSAK 236 (para. 2), so they take no share of the loss.</p><blockquote><p>Impairment loss = (400,000 + 50,000 + 30,000) − 420,000 = 60,000</p><p>To goodwill = 50,000; to oil and gas properties = 60,000 − 50,000 = 10,000</p><p>Dr Impairment loss on non-financial assets 60,000 / Cr Goodwill (accumulated impairment) 50,000, Cr Allowance for impairment, oil and gas properties 10,000</p></blockquote><p>If prices recover, the 10,000 may be reversed up to its ceiling, but the 50,000 never is (PSAK 236.124), because a later rise in the unit's value may be internally generated goodwill.</p><h2>8. Intangible assets across industries</h2><p><strong>Telecommunications.</strong> A mobile operator's up-front spectrum licence fee is amortised over the licence term, and annual frequency usage fees are expensed as they fall due. For example (illustration), an up-front fee of 1,000 for a ten-year licence is amortised at 1,000 / 10 = 100 a year.</p><p><strong>Pharmaceuticals.</strong> Feasibility and benefits are hard to show before a regulator approves a drug, so drug makers commonly expense development until approval. A compound bought in mid-development is capitalised at cost, because a purchase always meets the recognition test (para. 25).</p><p><strong>Toll roads and power concessions.</strong> Under ISAK 112 (formerly ISAK 16, IFRIC 12), an operator that builds infrastructure for a government that controls its use, its prices and the asset at the end does not recognise the road as a fixed asset. It recognises an intangible right to charge users when it bears traffic risk, or a financial asset when the grantor guarantees the cash. For example (illustration), a toll road built for 5,000 under a 40-year concession (intangible measured at the fair value of the construction service, here assumed equal to cost) is amortised at 5,000 / 40 = 125 a year straight-line, or at 5,000 × 2% = 100 under a traffic-based method in a first year carrying 2 percent of expected total traffic. A toll-revenue method is presumed inappropriate (para. 98A). A power producer selling to PLN, the state electricity company, may fall under ISAK 112 or the lease rules, depending on who controls the plant.</p><p><strong>Plantations.</strong> Plantation land is held through <em>hak guna usaha</em> (HGU), a cultivation right of limited term. The right is tested under ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36; see the Fixed Assets essay), and extension costs have commonly been carried as amortised intangibles. For tax, the Income Tax Law lists the costs of extending HGB and HGU, and goodwill, among expenditure amortised in four-, eight-, sixteen- or twenty-year groups, or, since UU HPP (2021), over the actual useful life where it exceeds twenty years (UU PPh art. 11A(2), (2a); PMK 72/2023).</p><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 13</h2><p>PHE gives the movement table PSAK 238.118 asks for (Note 13, Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 December 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accumulated amortisation</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Land rights</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>563</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(315)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>248</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Software and licences</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>93,921</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(75,691)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>18,230</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>113,470</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(26,755)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>86,715</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allowance for impairment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(33,379)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Intangible assets, net</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>207,954</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(102,761)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>71,814</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Opening 73,800 + additions 14,038 − carrying amount derecognised (5,580 − 2,768) − net correction (2,257 − 2,195) − amortisation 13,150 = 71,814</p></blockquote><p>Goodwill dominates the line. PHE does not split the allowance of 33,379 by class. If all of it is the fully impaired Nunukan goodwill, the remaining ONWJ goodwill is 113,470 − 26,755 − 33,379 = 53,336, or 74.3 percent of the line, and software and land rights make up the other 18,478.</p><p><strong>Goodwill and land rights in Note 13.</strong> Goodwill arose from the 2009 acquisition of ONWJ Ltd. and the 2013 acquisition of PHE Nunukan Company, whose goodwill was fully impaired in 2021; when the Nunukan block was impaired by a further 53,303 in 2023, the Note 17 table's goodwill column showed none for it (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129). On the 53,336 reading ONWJ's gross goodwill is 80,091, and the 26,755 of accumulated amortisation, unchanged in both years, is 33.4 percent of it, which fits amortisation charged before the revised PSAK 22 took effect in 2011. If, as secondary sources say, the revision required that amortisation to be eliminated against cost, PHE's gross presentation does not follow it, though net goodwill is unchanged. Land rights are HGB with terms ending between 2024 and 2045, amortised although management expects renewal, at a 2024 charge of 26 on average cost of 576.5, about 22 years. Since policy 3i sends initial legal costs to land, the amortised intangible is most likely extension costs, the treatment of the former ISAK 25.</p><blockquote><p>Software amortisation rate = 13,124 / ((87,693 + 93,921) / 2) = 14.5% (2024); 6,505 / ((82,412 + 87,693) / 2) = 7.6% (2023)</p><p>Accumulated amortisation / cost, software = 75,691 / 93,921 = 80.6% (77.0% in 2023)</p></blockquote><p>On straight-line, 14.5 percent implies an average life of about seven years and 7.6 percent about thirteen, measured on gross cost that still includes fully amortised software, so the life of assets still being amortised is shorter. Amortisation doubled from 6,532 to 13,150 while software cost rose 7.1 percent in 2024, and four-fifths of the software's cost is already consumed.</p><p>The FS do not show useful lives, the amortisation method or the line item carrying amortisation for software and land rights (para. 118(a), (b), (d)), so the doubling cannot be explained. The policies cover only goodwill (Note 3d), and the DD&amp;A line in profit or loss cites Notes 12, 16a and 17 but not 13 (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14). The "correction" columns (−2,230 of software cost, +2,181 of accumulated amortisation) are unexplained. In 2023 software deductions removed 865 of cost but 875 of accumulated amortisation, a negative carrying amount of 10. The critical estimates note calls "goodwill or intangible assets not ready to use" examples of indefinite useful life (Daftar 5/82, PDF p. 99, both language columns), but an asset not yet ready for use is tested annually because amortisation has not started, not because its life is indefinite.</p><h2>10. Upstream note</h2><p>Under PHE's cost recovery production sharing contracts (PSCs), materials and equipment bought for petroleum operations belong to the Government (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). The FS do not say whether software falls under that clause. The goodwill here arose because PHE bought companies holding PSC interests (ONWJ Ltd. and PHE Nunukan Company).</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">oil-gas-properties-net</a>: exploration and evaluation assets, PSC interests, and the CGUs that carry goodwill.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>: software integral to hardware, and initial legal costs of land rights.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net">right-of-use-assets-net</a>: land rights that are leases in substance.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">depreciation-depletion-amortization</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/impairment-non-financial-assets">impairment-non-financial-assets</a>: where amortisation and impairment are presumably charged.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments">long-term-investments</a>: goodwill inside associates.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: deferred tax on fair value step-ups.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That the allowance of 33,379 belongs wholly to Nunukan goodwill, so that ONWJ goodwill is 53,336 net, is an inference. Note 13 does not split the allowance, and the FS do not give each acquisition's original goodwill.</p></li><li><p>That the 26,755 of accumulated amortisation predates the revised PSAK 22 is inferred from the 2009 acquisition date and the 33.4 percent ratio. PSAK 22 (2010), effective 1 January 2011, is reported in secondary sources to have required eliminating accumulated amortisation against goodwill cost on transition, as IFRS 3 (2004) did; PHE still presents the 26,755 gross. The transition paragraph itself was not opened.</p></li><li><p>The reading that PHE's intangible land rights are extension costs, as the former ISAK 25 is understood to have required, reconciles Note 13 with policy 3i, but PHE does not say so. No text of ISAK 25 or of ISAK 336's paragraph on renewal costs (DK09) could be found.</p></li><li><p>The industry paragraphs describe common practice under the standards, not checked against any named company's statements.</p></li><li><p>Expensing patent defence costs and trademark registration fees under PSAK 238, against Kieso's capitalisation, is this page's reading of paragraphs 20 and 63; neither paragraph names those costs.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); statement of profit or loss (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 3d (Daftar 5/35–5/37, PDF pp. 52–54); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69); Note 3l (Daftar 5/54, PDF p. 71); Note 3m(i) (Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73); Note 3w (Daftar 5/72–5/73, PDF pp. 89–90); critical estimates (Daftar 5/82, PDF p. 99); Note 13 (Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121); Note 17 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). Indonesian and English columns read for Notes 3d, 3w, 13 and the critical estimates. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition (Wiley), chapter on intangible assets. Not opened; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 238 <em>Aset Takberwujud</em> (IAS 38 <em>Intangible Assets</em>), paras. 2–4, 7–8, 11–13, 17, 20, 24–27, 33–35, 45–46, 52–54, 56–57, 59, 63–67, 71–72, 75, 88–90, 94, 96–98C, 100, 104, 107–109, 118. VERIFIED (web, AASB 138 August 2015, legislation.gov.au F2015L01558, which reproduces IAS 38 paragraph for paragraph). Derecognition (paras. 112–113): STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 236 <em>Penurunan Nilai Aset</em> (IAS 36): annual tests for goodwill and for indefinite-life and not-yet-available intangibles, allocation to CGUs, no reversal of goodwill impairment, as restated in PHE Note 3w. VERIFIED (PHE FS). Paras. 104–105: VERIFIED (web, AASB 136 text; ACCA DipIFR, "Impairment of goodwill"). Para. 124: VERIFIED (web, secondary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 103 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis</em> (IFRS 3): acquisition method, NCI choice, acquisition costs expensed, bargain purchase reassessment, as restated in PHE Note 3d. VERIFIED (PHE FS). Standard text not opened: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decisions on cloud computing under IAS 38: customer's right to access the supplier's software (March 2019) and configuration or customisation costs (March 2021, approved by the IASB April 2021). VERIFIED (web, Grant Thornton IFRS Viewpoint; Queensland Audit Office). Indonesian application not checked.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 22 (1994) goodwill amortisation over 5–20 years and its replacement by impairment testing in the 2010 revision: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Jurnal JABIS, UII; Budi Luhur journal). Effective date 1 January 2011 and elimination of accumulated amortisation on transition: VERIFIED (web, secondary, Budi Luhur and UII JABIS; transition from one source). Standard text not opened.</p></li><li><p>ISAK 112 (IFRIC 12), ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36), PSAK 106 (IFRS 6), PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), PSAK 208 (IAS 8), PSAK 216 (IAS 16): numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em>). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36). ISAK 112's intangible and financial asset models VERIFIED (web, secondary: IMF GFSAC paper on IFRIC 12, as in the Fixed Assets essay). Section 8 industry practice: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>UU No. 7/1983 on Income Tax as amended by UU No. 36/2008, art. 11A (amortisation of intangibles, including costs of extending HGB, HGU and hak pakai, and goodwill, in 4-, 8-, 16- and 20-year groups). VERIFIED (web, pasal.id). Art. 11A(2a) added by UU No. 7/2021 (HPP), with PP 55/2022 art. 22(5) and PMK 72/2023: VERIFIED (web, secondary: pajak.com; IAI, <em>Sosialisasi PMK 72 Tahun 2023</em>; DDTCNews).</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An intangible asset is an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance. Identifiability, meaning separable or arising from a contract or legal right, is what divides it from goodwill.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bought intangibles are recorded at cost, or at fair value when acquired in a business combination. Internally generated ones face a stricter rule. Research is always expensed, development is capitalised only from the date six criteria are all met, and brands and customer lists built in-house are never recognised.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A finite-life intangible is amortised over the shorter of its economic and legal life, normally straight-line with zero residual value. An indefinite-life intangible and goodwill are not amortised but tested for impairment every year, and a goodwill impairment is never reversed.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report intangible assets, net, of 71,814 at 31 December 2024 against 73,800 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 0.24 percent of total assets and 0.32 percent of non-current assets (0.24 and 0.35 percent a year earlier). It holds software and licences, land rights, and goodwill from two acquisitions (Note 13, Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A student should be able to recognise, measure, amortise and impair an intangible, and compute and test goodwill, with journals.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standards are PSAK 238 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Aset Takberwujud","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 19, IAS 38) and, for goodwill, PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What an intangible asset is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 238 defines an intangible asset as an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance (para. 8). Kieso reduces this to four characteristics: identifiable, no physical form, benefits over more than one period, and not held for sale. Control normally comes from legal rights (para. 13), and benefits include cost savings (para. 17).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identifiability","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" separates intangible assets from goodwill (para. 11). An item is identifiable if it is ","type":"text"},{"text":"separable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", capable of being sold, transferred or licensed on its own, or if it arises from contractual or other legal rights, transferable or not (para. 12). A software licence is both. A trained workforce is neither, so it stays in goodwill.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso sorts intangibles into types, which help when an acquisition is broken down (section 7).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Type","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Examples","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Marketing-related","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trademarks, trade names, domain names","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customer-related","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customer lists, order backlogs, customer relationships","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Artistic-related","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Copyrights on books, music, pictures, video","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contract-related","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Franchises, licences, permits, concessions","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Technology-related","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Patents, software, databases, trade secrets","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The residual in a business combination (not identifiable)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Boundaries.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Software integral to hardware, such as a machine's operating system, is part of the hardware under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16) (para. 4). PSAK 238 excludes exploration and evaluation assets, which follow PSAK 106 (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6), and oil, gas and mineral development and extraction (para. 2), but not software used there (para. 7).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Acquired intangible assets","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A separately acquired intangible is measured at cost (para. 24). The recognition tests, probable benefits and reliable cost, are treated as met for a purchase, because the price reflects the buyer's expectation of benefit (paras. 25–26). Cost is the purchase price, including non-refundable taxes, less trade discounts, plus every directly attributable cost of preparing the asset for use, such as professional fees and testing (paras. 27–28). Training staff and general overhead are expensed (para. 29).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), within PHE's 2024 software additions of 14,038 (Note 13), suppose one item is a perpetual licence for reservoir simulation software, list price 2,000, with a 10 percent volume discount. The vendor charges 250 to install and integrate it, testing before go-live costs 120, training the engineers costs 80, and first-year support is 150, expensed over the support year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase price = 2,000 × (1 − 10%) = 1,800","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost = 1,800 + 250 installation + 120 testing = 2,170","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kept out of cost = 80 training (expensed) + 150 support (prepaid, expensed over the year) = 230","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Intangible assets, software 2,170, Dr General and administrative expenses 80, Dr Prepaid expenses 150 / Cr Trade payables 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An intangible received in exchange for a non-monetary asset is measured at fair value unless the exchange lacks commercial substance or neither fair value can be measured reliably (paras. 45–46), the rule the Fixed Assets essay works through. One acquired in a business combination is measured at acquisition-date fair value, separately from goodwill (paras. 33–34).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Internally generated intangibles: research and development","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No market price confirms the value of an intangible built in-house, so PSAK 238 divides each internal project into two phases to decide when spending starts creating an asset (para. 52).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Research","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is original and planned investigation to gain new scientific or technical knowledge (para. 8), such as searching for and evaluating alternatives (para. 56). No asset is recognised from it, and the spending is expensed when incurred (para. 54).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Development","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is applying research findings to a plan or design for new or substantially improved products, processes or systems before commercial use (para. 8), such as designing and testing prototypes (para. 59). It is capitalised only when the entity can show all six criteria (para. 57):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"technical feasibility of completing the asset;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the intention to complete it and use or sell it;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the ability to use or sell it;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"how it will generate probable future benefits, through a market or, if used internally, its usefulness;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"adequate technical, financial and other resources to finish it;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the ability to measure its cost reliably.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If research cannot be told apart from development, the whole project is treated as research (para. 53). Cost starts from the date the criteria are first met, and earlier spending stays in expense and cannot be reinstated (paras. 65, 71). Only directly attributable costs qualify; overhead and training do not (paras. 66–67). Under PSAK 238 personnel and contract-service costs follow the phase, capitalised in development once the criteria are met, and separately purchased items are measured at cost (para. 24). Materials and equipment with alternative future uses become inventory or fixed assets.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary builds production-surveillance software in 2024. From January to March it studies feasibility and evaluates vendor products (300) and codes a rough prototype (150). On 1 April the budget is approved and the design is shown to work, so all six criteria are met. From April to December it spends 700 on programmers, 260 on contract coders and 140 on testing, and is charged 90 of head-office overhead and 60 for training field operators.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Before 1 April (criteria not met)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"From 1 April (criteria met)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expensed","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"research 300 + development before criteria 150 = 450","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"90 overhead + 60 training = 150","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Capitalised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"none","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"700 + 260 + 140 = 1,100","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Research and development expense 450 / Cr Cash and accrued expenses 450","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Intangible assets, software under development 1,100, Dr General and administrative expenses 150 / Cr Cash and accrued expenses 1,250","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Of 1,700 spent, 1,100 becomes an asset. The 150 prototype is development work (para. 59), but it came before the criteria were met, so it stays in expense although it fed the final product (para. 71).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What is never capitalised.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Internally generated brands, mastheads, publishing titles and customer lists are not recognised, because their cost cannot be told apart from developing the business as a whole (paras. 63–64). PSAK 238.63 does not recognise an internally generated brand at all, so the attorney, registration, design, consulting and successful defence costs of a self-developed trademark are expensed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cloud software.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in 2019 that a software-as-a-service (SaaS) contract is usually a service, and in 2021 that configuring or customising the supplier's software is expensed as the service is received unless it creates an asset the customer controls. For example (illustration), a three-year SaaS contract for a production-accounting system costs 150 a year, and the supplier charges 90 up front for configuration that is not distinct from the subscription. The 150 is expensed each year, and the 90 is a prepayment expensed at 90 / 3 = 30 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The upstream parallel.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Geologists' work is an example of research, and PHE likewise expenses geological and geophysical costs under PSAK 106 (Note 3m(i), Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73; see the Oil and Gas Properties essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Amortisation","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"How an intangible is amortised depends on whether its useful life is finite or indefinite (paras. 88–89). An intangible with a finite life is amortised. One with an indefinite life, with no foreseeable limit to the period over which it will bring in net cash, is not (para. 107).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Useful life","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" reflects expected use, obsolescence, competitors' actions, maintenance needed and legal limits (para. 90). A right arising from a contract or law is amortised over no more than its term (para. 94). Renewal periods count only if renewal is evidenced, its conditions will be met and its cost is small relative to its benefits (para. 96).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Residual value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is assumed to be zero unless a third party has committed to buy the asset or an active market will exist at the end of its life (para. 100).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Method.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Amortisation starts when the asset is available for use and follows the pattern in which benefits are consumed, with straight-line when that pattern cannot be determined reliably (paras. 97–98). A revenue-based method is presumed inappropriate, because revenue reflects prices and volumes as well as use (para. 98A), unless the right itself is defined by a revenue limit (paras. 98B–98C). Period and method are reviewed each year end, and a change is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8) (para. 104).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the surveillance software goes live on 1 January 2025 with a five-year life. The simulation licence is perpetual, but the subsidiary expects to replace it after seven years as the technology dates.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Surveillance software = 1,100 / 5 = 220 a year; simulation licence = 2,170 / 7 = 310 a year","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Amortisation expense 530 / Cr Accumulated amortisation, software 530","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At the end of 2026 the surveillance software carries 1,100 − 2 × 220 = 660. If its remaining life is revised to two years, amortisation becomes 660 / 2 = 330 a year from 2027, with no restatement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land rights.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian land rights such as ","type":"text"},{"text":"hak guna bangunan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (HGB, a right to build) run for a term and can be extended. PHE's policy puts the initial legal costs of a land right into land, undepreciated, and treats rights that are leases in substance under PSAK 116 (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69; the Right-of-Use Assets essay explains the ISAK 336 test). Extension and renewal costs have a term of their own, so they are commonly carried as an intangible and amortised over the extended term. For example (illustration), fees of 60 to extend an HGB for 20 years are amortised at 60 / 20 = 3 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indefinite life.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For example (illustration), a subsidiary buys a trade name for 5,000, renewable every ten years at a small fee, with no plan to stop using it. It is not amortised but tested for impairment each year (para. 108), and the life assessment is reviewed each period (para. 109). If in 2027 management decides to retire the name in four years, it is tested for impairment and then amortised at 5,000 / 4 = 1,250 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Subsequent costs, revaluation and derecognition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Later spending is only rarely capitalised, because most of it maintains benefits already expected (para. 20). Under PSAK 238 the cost of successfully defending a patent keeps existing benefits and is normally expensed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An entity may choose the revaluation model for a class of intangibles only where fair value can be measured in an ","type":"text"},{"text":"active market","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (paras. 72, 75). Patents, brands and software are unique, so the model is rare. PHE uses cost.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An intangible is derecognised on disposal or when no further benefit is expected, with the gain or loss in profit or loss. For example (illustration), take PHE's 2024 software deductions (Note 13), cost 5,580 and accumulated amortisation 2,768, as systems retired with no proceeds.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Carrying amount = 5,580 − 2,768 = 2,812","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Accumulated amortisation, software 2,768, Dr Loss on derecognition 2,812 / Cr Intangible assets, software 5,580","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Impairment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An intangible is written down when its carrying amount exceeds its ","type":"text"},{"text":"recoverable amount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, under PSAK 236 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penurunan Nilai Aset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36). The mechanism is taught in the Fixed Assets and Oil and Gas Properties essays; intangibles differ in when the test is required.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Asset","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When tested","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reversal","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finite-life intangible","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When there is an indication of impairment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowed, up to the carrying amount with no impairment","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indefinite-life intangible","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every year, and on an indication","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowed, same cap","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not yet available for use","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowed, same cap","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every year, within a cash-generating unit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Never (PSAK 236.124)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The test is against recoverable amount, not an appraised fair value alone. Fair value less costs of disposal is only one leg; value in use may be higher when the asset is worth more in the entity's own use. PHE's policy lists the same annual tests (Note 3w, Daftar 5/72, PDF p. 89).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), suppose instead that the surveillance software served one field, and in late 2025 the subsidiary's contract for that field is not extended. The software cannot be sold, so fair value less costs of disposal is nil, and value in use for the remaining months is 250.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Carrying amount at 31 December 2025 = 1,100 − 220 = 880","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Impairment loss = 880 − higher of (0, 250) = 630","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Impairment loss on non-financial assets 630 / Cr Allowance for impairment, software 630","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Goodwill","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill is what a buyer pays beyond the fair value of the identifiable assets and liabilities it gets, such as the assembled workforce and expected synergies. It is not identifiable, so it falls outside PSAK 238 (para. 3(f)) and is measured under PSAK 103. Internally generated goodwill is never recognised.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Measurement.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Under the acquisition method the acquirer measures identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value at the acquisition date, including intangibles the acquiree never recorded. Goodwill is the residual. Acquisition costs are expensed; PHE puts them in general and administrative expenses (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill = consideration transferred + non-controlling interest (NCI) − fair value of identifiable net assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 100 percent of an oilfield-services company for 120,000 cash.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Book value","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fixed assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"48,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"61,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inventories","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customer contracts (order backlog)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"none","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Software","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liabilities","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(16,500)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(16,500)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"62,000","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"84,000","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill = 120,000 − 84,000 = 36,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Fixed assets 61,500, Dr Inventories 12,000, Dr Receivables 10,500, Dr Cash 7,500, Dr Intangible assets, customer contracts 6,000, Dr Intangible assets, software 3,000, Dr Goodwill 36,000 / Cr Liabilities 16,500, Cr Cash 120,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Of the 58,000 paid above book value, 22,000 is fair value step-ups, including 6,000 of customer contracts the seller never recorded, and 36,000 is goodwill. Deferred tax on the step-ups is ignored here; PHE carries a deferred tax liability of 7,760 labelled \"excess fair value over NBV\" (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-controlling interest.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When less than 100 percent is bought, PSAK 103 lets the acquirer measure NCI, acquisition by acquisition, at fair value or at its proportionate share of identifiable net assets, a choice PHE's policy states (Note 3d). Suppose the subsidiary buys 80 percent for 96,000, and the other 20 percent has a fair value of 23,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at proportionate share","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at fair value","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"20% × 84,000 = 16,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"96,000 + 16,800 − 84,000 = 28,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"96,000 + 23,000 − 84,000 = 35,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Whose goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent's only","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent's and the NCI's","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bargain purchase.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A price below the fair value of net assets is first rechecked, and any excess left is a gain in profit or loss. Bought for 80,000, the company above gives a gain of 84,000 − 80,000 = 4,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No amortisation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 22 (1994) amortised goodwill over five to twenty years; the revision that adopted IFRS 3 replaced amortisation with an annual impairment test. PHE's Note 13 still shows the old amortisation (section 9).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Impairment of goodwill.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Goodwill brings in no cash on its own, so from the acquisition date it is allocated to the cash-generating units (CGUs) expected to benefit from the combination (PHE Note 3d, Daftar 5/37, PDF p. 54), and each is tested every year (Note 3w, Daftar 5/73, PDF p. 90). A unit's impairment loss goes first to goodwill and then to its other assets pro rata to carrying amount (PSAK 236.104), with no asset taken below the highest of its fair value less costs of disposal, value in use and zero (para. 105). For example (illustration), a one-block CGU carries oil and gas properties of 400,000, goodwill of 50,000 and current assets of 30,000, and lower price forecasts cut its recoverable amount to 420,000. The current assets (receivables and inventories) are measured under their own standards and sit outside PSAK 236 (para. 2), so they take no share of the loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Impairment loss = (400,000 + 50,000 + 30,000) − 420,000 = 60,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"To goodwill = 50,000; to oil and gas properties = 60,000 − 50,000 = 10,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Impairment loss on non-financial assets 60,000 / Cr Goodwill (accumulated impairment) 50,000, Cr Allowance for impairment, oil and gas properties 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If prices recover, the 10,000 may be reversed up to its ceiling, but the 50,000 never is (PSAK 236.124), because a later rise in the unit's value may be internally generated goodwill.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Intangible assets across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Telecommunications.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A mobile operator's up-front spectrum licence fee is amortised over the licence term, and annual frequency usage fees are expensed as they fall due. For example (illustration), an up-front fee of 1,000 for a ten-year licence is amortised at 1,000 / 10 = 100 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pharmaceuticals.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Feasibility and benefits are hard to show before a regulator approves a drug, so drug makers commonly expense development until approval. A compound bought in mid-development is capitalised at cost, because a purchase always meets the recognition test (para. 25).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Toll roads and power concessions.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Under ISAK 112 (formerly ISAK 16, IFRIC 12), an operator that builds infrastructure for a government that controls its use, its prices and the asset at the end does not recognise the road as a fixed asset. It recognises an intangible right to charge users when it bears traffic risk, or a financial asset when the grantor guarantees the cash. For example (illustration), a toll road built for 5,000 under a 40-year concession (intangible measured at the fair value of the construction service, here assumed equal to cost) is amortised at 5,000 / 40 = 125 a year straight-line, or at 5,000 × 2% = 100 under a traffic-based method in a first year carrying 2 percent of expected total traffic. A toll-revenue method is presumed inappropriate (para. 98A). A power producer selling to PLN, the state electricity company, may fall under ISAK 112 or the lease rules, depending on who controls the plant.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plantations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Plantation land is held through ","type":"text"},{"text":"hak guna usaha","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (HGU), a cultivation right of limited term. The right is tested under ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36; see the Fixed Assets essay), and extension costs have commonly been carried as amortised intangibles. For tax, the Income Tax Law lists the costs of extending HGB and HGU, and goodwill, among expenditure amortised in four-, eight-, sixteen- or twenty-year groups, or, since UU HPP (2021), over the actual useful life where it exceeds twenty years (UU PPh art. 11A(2), (2a); PMK 72/2023).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 13","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE gives the movement table PSAK 238.118 asks for (Note 13, Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accumulated amortisation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land rights","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"563","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(315)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"248","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Software and licences","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"93,921","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(75,691)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"18,230","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"113,470","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(26,755)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"86,715","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowance for impairment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(33,379)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Intangible assets, net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"207,954","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(102,761)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"71,814","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening 73,800 + additions 14,038 − carrying amount derecognised (5,580 − 2,768) − net correction (2,257 − 2,195) − amortisation 13,150 = 71,814","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill dominates the line. PHE does not split the allowance of 33,379 by class. If all of it is the fully impaired Nunukan goodwill, the remaining ONWJ goodwill is 113,470 − 26,755 − 33,379 = 53,336, or 74.3 percent of the line, and software and land rights make up the other 18,478.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill and land rights in Note 13.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Goodwill arose from the 2009 acquisition of ONWJ Ltd. and the 2013 acquisition of PHE Nunukan Company, whose goodwill was fully impaired in 2021; when the Nunukan block was impaired by a further 53,303 in 2023, the Note 17 table's goodwill column showed none for it (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129). On the 53,336 reading ONWJ's gross goodwill is 80,091, and the 26,755 of accumulated amortisation, unchanged in both years, is 33.4 percent of it, which fits amortisation charged before the revised PSAK 22 took effect in 2011. If, as secondary sources say, the revision required that amortisation to be eliminated against cost, PHE's gross presentation does not follow it, though net goodwill is unchanged. Land rights are HGB with terms ending between 2024 and 2045, amortised although management expects renewal, at a 2024 charge of 26 on average cost of 576.5, about 22 years. Since policy 3i sends initial legal costs to land, the amortised intangible is most likely extension costs, the treatment of the former ISAK 25.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Software amortisation rate = 13,124 / ((87,693 + 93,921) / 2) = 14.5% (2024); 6,505 / ((82,412 + 87,693) / 2) = 7.6% (2023)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accumulated amortisation / cost, software = 75,691 / 93,921 = 80.6% (77.0% in 2023)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On straight-line, 14.5 percent implies an average life of about seven years and 7.6 percent about thirteen, measured on gross cost that still includes fully amortised software, so the life of assets still being amortised is shorter. Amortisation doubled from 6,532 to 13,150 while software cost rose 7.1 percent in 2024, and four-fifths of the software's cost is already consumed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The FS do not show useful lives, the amortisation method or the line item carrying amortisation for software and land rights (para. 118(a), (b), (d)), so the doubling cannot be explained. The policies cover only goodwill (Note 3d), and the DD&A line in profit or loss cites Notes 12, 16a and 17 but not 13 (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14). The \"correction\" columns (−2,230 of software cost, +2,181 of accumulated amortisation) are unexplained. In 2023 software deductions removed 865 of cost but 875 of accumulated amortisation, a negative carrying amount of 10. The critical estimates note calls \"goodwill or intangible assets not ready to use\" examples of indefinite useful life (Daftar 5/82, PDF p. 99, both language columns), but an asset not yet ready for use is tested annually because amortisation has not started, not because its life is indefinite.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PHE's cost recovery production sharing contracts (PSCs), materials and equipment bought for petroleum operations belong to the Government (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). The FS do not say whether software falls under that clause. The goodwill here arose because PHE bought companies holding PSC interests (ONWJ Ltd. and PHE Nunukan Company).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"oil-gas-properties-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": exploration and evaluation assets, PSC interests, and the CGUs that carry goodwill.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": software integral to hardware, and initial legal costs of land rights.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"right-of-use-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": land rights that are leases in substance.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"depreciation-depletion-amortization","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"impairment-non-financial-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/impairment-non-financial-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where amortisation and impairment are presumably charged.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-investments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": goodwill inside associates.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": deferred tax on fair value step-ups.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the allowance of 33,379 belongs wholly to Nunukan goodwill, so that ONWJ goodwill is 53,336 net, is an inference. Note 13 does not split the allowance, and the FS do not give each acquisition's original goodwill.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the 26,755 of accumulated amortisation predates the revised PSAK 22 is inferred from the 2009 acquisition date and the 33.4 percent ratio. PSAK 22 (2010), effective 1 January 2011, is reported in secondary sources to have required eliminating accumulated amortisation against goodwill cost on transition, as IFRS 3 (2004) did; PHE still presents the 26,755 gross. The transition paragraph itself was not opened.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reading that PHE's intangible land rights are extension costs, as the former ISAK 25 is understood to have required, reconciles Note 13 with policy 3i, but PHE does not say so. No text of ISAK 25 or of ISAK 336's paragraph on renewal costs (DK09) could be found.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The industry paragraphs describe common practice under the standards, not checked against any named company's statements.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expensing patent defence costs and trademark registration fees under PSAK 238, against Kieso's capitalisation, is this page's reading of paragraphs 20 and 63; neither paragraph names those costs.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); statement of profit or loss (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 3d (Daftar 5/35–5/37, PDF pp. 52–54); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69); Note 3l (Daftar 5/54, PDF p. 71); Note 3m(i) (Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73); Note 3w (Daftar 5/72–5/73, PDF pp. 89–90); critical estimates (Daftar 5/82, PDF p. 99); Note 13 (Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121); Note 17 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). Indonesian and English columns read for Notes 3d, 3w, 13 and the critical estimates. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition (Wiley), chapter on intangible assets. Not opened; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 238 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Aset Takberwujud","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 38 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intangible Assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), paras. 2–4, 7–8, 11–13, 17, 20, 24–27, 33–35, 45–46, 52–54, 56–57, 59, 63–67, 71–72, 75, 88–90, 94, 96–98C, 100, 104, 107–109, 118. VERIFIED (web, AASB 138 August 2015, legislation.gov.au F2015L01558, which reproduces IAS 38 paragraph for paragraph). Derecognition (paras. 112–113): STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 236 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penurunan Nilai Aset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 36): annual tests for goodwill and for indefinite-life and not-yet-available intangibles, allocation to CGUs, no reversal of goodwill impairment, as restated in PHE Note 3w. VERIFIED (PHE FS). Paras. 104–105: VERIFIED (web, AASB 136 text; ACCA DipIFR, \"Impairment of goodwill\"). Para. 124: VERIFIED (web, secondary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 3): acquisition method, NCI choice, acquisition costs expensed, bargain purchase reassessment, as restated in PHE Note 3d. VERIFIED (PHE FS). Standard text not opened: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decisions on cloud computing under IAS 38: customer's right to access the supplier's software (March 2019) and configuration or customisation costs (March 2021, approved by the IASB April 2021). VERIFIED (web, Grant Thornton IFRS Viewpoint; Queensland Audit Office). Indonesian application not checked.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 22 (1994) goodwill amortisation over 5–20 years and its replacement by impairment testing in the 2010 revision: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Jurnal JABIS, UII; Budi Luhur journal). Effective date 1 January 2011 and elimination of accumulated amortisation on transition: VERIFIED (web, secondary, Budi Luhur and UII JABIS; transition from one source). Standard text not opened.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ISAK 112 (IFRIC 12), ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36), PSAK 106 (IFRS 6), PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), PSAK 208 (IAS 8), PSAK 216 (IAS 16): numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36). ISAK 112's intangible and financial asset models VERIFIED (web, secondary: IMF GFSAC paper on IFRIC 12, as in the Fixed Assets essay). Section 8 industry practice: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"UU No. 7/1983 on Income Tax as amended by UU No. 36/2008, art. 11A (amortisation of intangibles, including costs of extending HGB, HGU and hak pakai, and goodwill, in 4-, 8-, 16- and 20-year groups). VERIFIED (web, pasal.id). Art. 11A(2a) added by UU No. 7/2021 (HPP), with PP 55/2022 art. 22(5) and PMK 72/2023: VERIFIED (web, secondary: pajak.com; IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Sosialisasi PMK 72 Tahun 2023","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; DDTCNews).","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of intangible assets from definition to goodwill impairment, worked through PHE's software, land rights and goodwill and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["An intangible asset is an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance. Identifiability, meaning separable or arising from a contract or legal right, is what divides it from goodwill.","Bought intangibles are recorded at cost, or at fair value when acquired in a business combination. Internally generated ones face a stricter rule. Research is always expensed, development is capitalised only from the date six criteria are all met, and brands and customer lists built in-house are never recognised.","A finite-life intangible is amortised over the shorter of its economic and legal life, normally straight-line with zero residual value. An indefinite-life intangible and goodwill are not amortised but tested for impairment every year, and a goodwill impairment is never reversed."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '29be61c1-9966-4d98-906b-b522bf8f8108' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
INSERT INTO public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
SELECT e.id, coalesce((SELECT max(x.revision_no) FROM public.essay_revisions x WHERE x.essay_id = e.id), 0) + 1, 'migration',
       e.title, e.snippet, e.content_json, e.status, $s$Lecture-notes source removed and references renumbered (site cleanup 2026-10, WP3)$s$, null
  FROM public.essays e WHERE e.id = '29be61c1-9966-4d98-906b-b522bf8f8108';

-- intercompany-profit-in-inventory
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A group earns revenue only when goods leave it. Consolidation therefore removes every intercompany sale and purchase in full, and removes the seller's profit on goods still held by another group company at year end, until those goods are sold to an outsider.</p></li><li><p>Who bears the deferred profit depends on direction. Profit on a downstream sale (parent to subsidiary) falls wholly on the parent's owners. Profit on an upstream sale (subsidiary to parent) belongs to a subsidiary that outside owners share in, so it is divided between the parent and the non-controlling interest (NCI).</p></li><li><p>The elimination changes the group's carrying amount of inventory but not its tax base, because Indonesian companies are taxed one by one. The difference is a deductible temporary difference under PSAK 212, so each deferral of profit carries a deferred tax asset at the buyer's tax rate, if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) eliminated inter-segment revenue of 4,538 in 2025 (5,136 in 2024), in USD thousand, to arrive at consolidated revenue of 7,630,662 (BRPT FS 2025, Note 46, PDF p. 239). Its policy states that "all intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation" (Note 3c, PDF pp. 67–68).</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to eliminate intercompany sales and unrealised profit in a consolidation worksheet, release last year's deferral, attribute both between the parent and NCI, compute the parent's equity-method income, and add the deferred tax.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. Why the group cannot profit from selling to itself</h2><p>A sale between two group companies moves goods from one pocket of the same owner to another, so from the group's point of view nothing has been sold. The usual justification is that the parent's management controls every intercompany transaction, including authorisation and pricing, and that the group is one reporting entity. A transfer price the parent sets itself cannot be evidence of value.</p><p>PSAK 110 <em>Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian</em> (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) para. B86(c) requires the group to "eliminate in full intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows", and adds that profits or losses on intragroup transactions "recognised in assets, such as inventory and fixed assets, are eliminated in full". Revenue in the consolidated statements is therefore earned only when a group company sells to an <em>outside party</em>, any entity that is not the parent or one of its subsidiaries.</p><p>The NCI essay (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest">non-controlling-interest</a>) covers how control is tested, how NCI is measured at acquisition and how profit, OCI and dividends are attributed to it. This page takes all of that as given and adds one complication. The subsidiary's profit "as the group sees it" excludes profit that is still sitting in another group company's warehouse.</p><h2>2. Eliminating intercompany sales and purchases</h2><p>Both group companies record an intercompany sale as if it were with an outsider, so the consolidated totals of sales and cost of sales are each overstated by the transfer price. The <em>transfer price</em> is the price one group company charges another.</p><p>For example (illustration), a parent sells goods that cost it 100,000 to its subsidiary for 120,000, a markup of 20 percent on cost. During the year the subsidiary sells goods that cost it 84,000 (at transfer price) to outside customers for 105,000. The remaining 36,000 is in its warehouse at year end.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Seller's books</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Buyer's books</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Purchase from outsider</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventory 100,000 / Cr Accounts payable 100,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Intercompany sale</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Due from subsidiary 120,000 / Cr Sales 120,000; Dr Cost of sales 100,000 / Cr Inventory 100,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventory 120,000 / Cr Due to parent 120,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sale to outsiders</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Accounts receivable 105,000 / Cr Sales 105,000; Dr Cost of sales 84,000 / Cr Inventory 84,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Gross profit in the separate books = (120,000 − 100,000) + (105,000 − 84,000) = 20,000 + 21,000 = 41,000</p><p>Group cost of goods sold to outsiders = 84,000 × 100 / 120 = 70,000</p><p>Group gross profit = 105,000 − 70,000 = 35,000</p></blockquote><p>The first elimination removes the intercompany sale from both sides.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Sales 120,000 / Cr Cost of sales 120,000</p></blockquote><p>The entry is the same whatever happened to the goods afterwards. It changes no profit, only the size of the two lines. Without it, the group would report sales of 225,000 and cost of sales of 184,000, and 120,000 of that revenue, 53 percent, would be sales to itself. An unpaid balance of 120,000 would also be eliminated as a reciprocal balance (K2, entry (6)).</p><h2>3. Unrealised profit in ending inventory</h2><p>Removing the sale leaves a gap of 41,000 − 35,000 = 6,000 between the separate books and the group. That gap is the seller's profit on goods the buyer still holds. Profit on an intercompany sale is <em>unrealised</em> while the goods remain inside the group, and <em>realised</em> when a group company sells them to an outsider.</p><blockquote><p>Ending inventory at transfer price = 36,000; at group cost = 36,000 × 100 / 120 = 30,000</p><p>Unrealised profit = 36,000 − 30,000 = 6,000</p><p>Dr Cost of sales 6,000 / Cr Inventory 6,000</p></blockquote><p>After the first entry, consolidated cost of sales is 100,000 + 84,000 − 120,000 = 64,000. The debit brings it to 70,000, the group's cost of the goods sold to outsiders, and the credit brings inventory down to what the group paid outsiders. Consolidated inventory is then 30,000, measured at group cost, as PSAK 202 (formerly PSAK 14, IAS 2) requires for any inventory.</p><p><strong>Markup on cost or margin on selling price.</strong> Intercompany pricing is usually quoted one of two ways, and confusing them misstates the elimination.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markup on cost (20%)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross margin on selling price (20%)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Transfer price of 100 of cost</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>100 × 1.20 = 120</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>100 / 0.80 = 125</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Unrealised profit as a share of the transfer price</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20 / 120 = 16.67%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20%</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Unrealised profit in 36,000 of ending inventory</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>36,000 × 16.67% = 6,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>36,000 × 20% = 7,200</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The rule is that the unrealised profit is always computed on the ending inventory <em>at transfer price</em>, using the profit as a fraction of the transfer price. A markup <em>m</em> on cost converts to a margin of <em>m</em> / (1 + <em>m</em>), so 20 percent on cost is 16.67 percent of price.</p><h2>4. Realising last year's deferral</h2><p>Profit deferred at the end of one year is realised in the next, when the buyer sells the goods. The buyer's opening inventory still carries the transfer price, so its cost of sales this year is overstated by the profit eliminated last year.</p><p>For example (illustration), the subsidiary opened the year with a further 24,000 of goods bought from the parent at the same 20 percent markup, all sold during the year (their cost of sales is left out of section 3's figures).</p><blockquote><p>Unrealised profit in opening inventory = 24,000 × 20 / 120 = 4,000</p></blockquote><p>This year's worksheet starts from the separate books, which never saw last year's elimination, so the deferral has to be repeated against opening equity and then released through cost of sales. With the parent on the <em>equity method</em> (it carries the investment at cost plus its share of the subsidiary's profit, less dividends; section 6), last year's deferral sits in the parent's investment account, so for a downstream sale the investment is debited.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Investment in subsidiary 4,000 / Cr Cost of sales 4,000</p></blockquote><p>The credit lowers cost of sales, so this year's group profit rises by 4,000, the profit earned when the goods finally left the group. The debit restores the investment account, which the parent had reduced by 4,000 last year, so the later elimination of the investment against the subsidiary's equity balances. If the parent carries the subsidiary at cost in its separate statements, as PSAK 227 (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows, the same debit goes to opening retained earnings instead (K2 explains the difference).</p><p>Over two years the profit is not lost, only moved into the year the goods were sold outside.</p><h2>5. Downstream and upstream sales</h2><p>Direction decides who bears the elimination. A <em>downstream</em> sale runs from parent to subsidiary, so the unrealised profit sits in the parent's own profit and falls wholly on the parent's owners. An <em>upstream</em> sale runs from subsidiary to parent, so the unrealised profit sits in the subsidiary's profit, which outside shareholders share. The elimination of sales, cost of sales and inventory is the same in both cases. Only the attribution differs.</p><p>The starting numbers are BRPT's. BRPT owns 34.63 percent of PT Chandra Asri Pacific Tbk (CAP) directly and 3.92 percent through its wholly owned Marigold Resources (Note 1, PDF pp. 24 and 28).</p><blockquote><p>Parent's effective interest = 34.63% + 3.92% = 38.55%; NCI = 100% − 38.55% = 61.45%</p></blockquote><p>BRPT consolidates CAP despite holding under half of it because, it judges, the composition of CAP's Board of Directors gives it power over CAP's relevant activities and variable returns (Note 4, PDF p. 112). The NCI essay explains the control test.</p><p>CAP reported revenue of 7,020,007, expenses of 5,572,021 and profit of 1,447,986 for 2025, before intragroup eliminations (Note 34, PDF p. 221). Use that profit as the subsidiary's reported profit, and assume (illustration) that the parent's separate profit before investment income is 200,000 and that the section 2–4 sales run between the two, with opening unrealised profit of 4,000 and closing unrealised profit of 6,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Downstream (parent sells)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Upstream (subsidiary sells)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Subsidiary's realised profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,447,986 (unaffected)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,447,986 + 4,000 − 6,000 = 1,445,986</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI share of profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>61.45% × 1,447,986 = 889,787</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>61.45% × 1,445,986 = 888,558</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Parent's income from subsidiary</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>38.55% × 1,447,986 + 4,000 − 6,000 = 556,199</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>38.55% × 1,445,986 = 557,428</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit attributable to owners of the parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200,000 + 556,199 = 756,199</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200,000 + 557,428 = 757,428</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consolidated profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200,000 + 1,447,986 − 2,000 = 1,645,986</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,645,986</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Consolidated profit is the same, because the same 2,000 of net deferral (6,000 − 4,000) leaves the group either way. The split moves. Downstream, the parent's owners absorb all 2,000. Upstream, they absorb 38.55 percent of it, 771, and NCI absorbs 1,229, which is the 757,428 − 756,199 gap.</p><blockquote><p>NCI share of profit (upstream) = NCI % × (subsidiary's reported profit + opening upstream unrealised profit − closing upstream unrealised profit)</p></blockquote><p><strong>What PSAK says about the split.</strong> PSAK 110 requires full elimination (B86(c)) and attribution of profit to the parent and NCI (B94), but does not say how an upstream elimination is attributed. Two methods are in use: the proportional method, and charging upstream profit wholly to the parent, as for downstream. IFRS 10 leaves the split to accounting policy. Proportional attribution is common and reflects that the profit being deferred is the subsidiary's, and this page and the NCI essay follow it.</p><h2>6. The parent's equity method and the worksheet</h2><p>Under the equity method the parent's own profit already equals the consolidated profit attributable to its owners, which is why it is called a one-line consolidation. The parent therefore has to make the same deferrals in its investment account.</p><blockquote><p>Income from subsidiary = parent % × subsidiary's reported profit + opening unrealised profit released − closing unrealised profit deferred (downstream in full; upstream × parent %)</p></blockquote><p>For the upstream case of section 5, the parent records:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Investment in subsidiary 557,428 / Cr Income from subsidiary 557,428</p></blockquote><p>Under PSAK the equity method is mainly a worksheet device for subsidiaries. In its separate statements a parent may carry a subsidiary at cost, under PSAK 109 or by the equity method (PSAK 227 para. 10).</p><p>The consolidation worksheet for the upstream case uses K2's numbering, with the profit eliminations between entries (5) and (6). They could equally come first; the order changes no total. This illustration has no fair value excess, so entries (4) and (5) are empty. Assume (illustration) that the parent's sales are 1,000,000, its cost of sales 700,000 and other expenses 100,000; that CAP's 5,572,021 of expenses splits into 5,000,000 of cost of sales and 572,021 of other expenses; and that the subsidiary paid no dividend.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Debit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Credit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1) Parent's income from subsidiary</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Income from subsidiary 557,428</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investment in subsidiary 557,428</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2) NCI share of profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI share of profit 888,558</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI 888,558</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3) Opening equity of subsidiary</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>as in K2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>as in K2</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5a) Intercompany sales</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sales 120,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost of sales 120,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5b) Opening unrealised profit, upstream</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investment in subsidiary 1,542 (38.55% × 4,000); NCI 2,458 (61.45% × 4,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost of sales 4,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5c) Closing unrealised profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost of sales 6,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Inventory 6,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6) Reciprocal balances</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>as in K2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>as in K2</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Entries (1), (2) and (5a)–(5c): debits = 557,428 + 888,558 + 120,000 + 1,542 + 2,458 + 6,000 = 1,575,986; credits = 557,428 + 888,558 + 120,000 + 4,000 + 6,000 = 1,575,986</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Income statement</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Parent</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Subsidiary</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Eliminations</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consolidated</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sales</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,000,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,020,007</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5a) −120,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,900,007</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost of sales</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(700,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5,000,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5a) +120,000, (5b) +4,000, (5c) −6,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5,582,000)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other expenses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(100,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(572,021)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(672,021)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Income from subsidiary</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>557,428</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1) −557,428</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>757,428</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,447,986</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,645,986</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI share of profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2) −888,558</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(888,558)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Attributable to owners of the parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>757,428</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>757,428</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The check is that the consolidated profit attributable to owners, 757,428, equals the parent's own profit under the equity method. Entry (5b) splits last year's deferral because the sale was upstream. The parent's 38.55 percent goes to the investment account and the NCI's 61.45 percent to opening NCI, since both were reduced when the profit was deferred. For a downstream sale, (5b) would debit the investment with the whole 4,000 and NCI would not appear.</p><h2>7. Deferred tax on eliminated profit</h2><p>The worksheet changes the group's carrying amount of inventory without changing anyone's tax position. BRPT states that "the Group is subject to income tax on an entity basis based on profit arising or derived from the tax jurisdiction in which the Group entities are domiciled and operates" (Note 23, PDF p. 145). Indonesia has no consolidated tax return, so the seller has already paid tax on the 6,000, and the buyer's <em>tax base</em> (the amount the tax authority will let it deduct) is the 36,000 it paid.</p><p>PSAK 212 <em>Pajak Penghasilan</em> (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) compares the consolidated carrying amount with that tax base. A carrying amount below the tax base is a <em>deductible temporary difference</em>, which gives a <em>deferred tax asset</em> (DTA), tax that will be recovered when the buyer sells the goods and deducts 36,000 against revenue the group sees as costing 30,000. PSAK 110 B86(c) points to it directly, saying the income-tax standard "applies to temporary differences that arise from the elimination of profits and losses resulting from intragroup transactions." Beams treats consolidated income taxes in a later chapter.</p><p>For example (illustration), both companies pay the general Indonesian corporate rate of 22 percent (whether CAP meets the listed-company conditions for 19 percent is not checked here).</p><blockquote><p>Temporary difference = tax base 36,000 − carrying amount 30,000 = 6,000</p><p>DTA = 6,000 × 22% = 1,320</p><p>Dr Deferred tax asset 1,320 / Cr Income tax expense 1,320</p><p>Net reduction in group profit after tax = 6,000 − 1,320 = 4,680</p></blockquote><p>The DTA is measured at the rate that will apply when the difference reverses, which is the buyer's rate, because the buyer holds the inventory and will take the deduction. When the two companies sit in different tax jurisdictions the seller's tax paid and the buyer's DTA no longer offset. CAP's group spans Indonesia and Singapore (Note 1, PDF p. 24). If the buyer were a Singapore company taxed at 17 percent:</p><blockquote><p>DTA = 6,000 × 17% = 1,020; tax paid by the Indonesian seller = 1,320; difference left in tax expense = 300</p></blockquote><p>Rates also differ inside Indonesia, and BRPT's own group shows it. Its geothermal JOC subsidiaries pay 34 percent (Note 23, PDF p. 145), so a DTA on inventory held by such a buyer would be 6,000 × 34% = 2,040. A listed company that meets the public-float conditions pays 3 points less than the general rate (19 percent), so the buyer's own status matters too.</p><p>Last year's deferral reverses with the goods. Its DTA of 4,000 × 22% = 880 was set up last year and is released this year, so for an upstream sale entry (5b) becomes:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Investment in subsidiary 1,203 (38.55% × (4,000 − 880)); Dr NCI 1,917 (61.45% × 3,120); Dr Income tax expense 880 / Cr Cost of sales 4,000</p><p>Net effect on this year's group profit = −6,000 + 4,000 + 1,320 − 880 = −1,560</p></blockquote><p>Upstream, NCI bears 61.45 percent of the after-tax effect and the parent 38.55 percent, so every percentage applied in section 5 is now applied to after-tax amounts. A DTA is recognised only if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it (PSAK 212 para. 24); BRPT, for instance, recognises none on the parent's fiscal losses (Note 41, PDF p. 225). A DTA can be offset only against a deferred tax liability of the same taxable entity, or of another entity that will settle net with the same authority, which Indonesia's entity-by-entity system rules out (PSAK 212 para. 74). That is why BRPT shows deferred tax assets of 122,742 and deferred tax liabilities of 263,652 for the CAP group side by side instead of netting them (Note 41, PDF p. 225).</p><p>US GAAP, which Beams follows, instead defers the seller's tax paid and forbids a buyer's-rate DTA on intra-entity inventory; PSAK has no such exception.</p><h2>8. Intercompany losses that signal impairment</h2><p>An intercompany sale below cost creates an unrealised loss, and the general rule eliminates it in the same way, by writing inventory back up to group cost. PSAK 110 B86(c) adds that "intragroup losses may indicate an impairment that requires recognition in the consolidated financial statements". If the loss exists because the goods are now worth less than they cost, the group would have had to write them down anyway under PSAK 202's lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV), so that part of the loss stays.</p><p>For example (illustration), a parent sells goods that cost it 10,000 to a subsidiary for 8,000. At year end they are still unsold and their NRV is 8,500.</p><blockquote><p>Loss in the parent's books = 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000</p><p>Write-down the group would recognise anyway = 10,000 − 8,500 = 1,500</p><p>Loss to eliminate = 8,500 − 8,000 = 500</p><p>Dr Inventory 500 / Cr Cost of sales 500</p></blockquote><p>Consolidated inventory is 8,500, the lower of group cost of 10,000 and NRV of 8,500. Eliminating the whole 2,000 would carry the goods at 10,000, above what they can be sold for.</p><h2>9. Intercompany profit across industries</h2><p><strong>Associates and joint ventures.</strong> An <em>associate</em> is an entity over which the investor has significant influence but not control, usually through a 20–50 percent holding, accounted for by the equity method. A sale to an associate is a sale to an outside party, because the associate is not in the group, so the revenue stays in full. Only the investor's share of the profit in goods still held is deferred (PSAK 228, formerly PSAK 15, IAS 28, para. 28). Losses that show a fall in NRV or an impairment are recognised in full for downstream sales and to the extent of the investor's share for upstream ones (para. 29).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Subsidiary</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Associate or joint venture</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sales and cost of sales</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>eliminated in full</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not eliminated</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Unrealised profit, downstream</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>eliminated in full, against the parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>eliminated to the investor's share</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Unrealised profit, upstream</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>eliminated in full, split parent / NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>eliminated to the investor's share</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry (illustration: 2,000 profit in goods held by a 45% associate)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Share of profit of associate 900 / Cr Investment in associate 900</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The IASB's 2024 exposure draft on the equity method proposes recognising these gains and losses in full. The IASB was still deliberating in 2026, so this row may change.</p><p><strong>Vertically integrated groups.</strong> Petrochemical producers moving olefins into polymer units, plantation groups selling crude palm oil to their own refineries, and coal miners supplying captive power plants all have large, recurring intercompany flows and inventory held at every stage. Segment reporting under PSAK 108 (formerly PSAK 5, IFRS 8) shows them when inter-segment revenue is part of the measure management reviews (para. 23(b)), together with the basis of accounting for transactions between segments (para. 27(a)).</p><p><strong>Investment entities.</strong> A parent that qualifies as an <em>investment entity</em> does not consolidate its investees but measures them at fair value through profit or loss (PSAK 110 para. 31). Sales to those investees are then sales to outsiders and are not eliminated.</p><p><strong>Transfer pricing.</strong> Consolidation eliminates the profit whatever the price, so an arm's-length tax adjustment to a transfer price changes current and deferred tax but not consolidated inventory, which stays at group cost.</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Note 46</h2><p>PSAK 108 asks for revenue from external customers and, where management reviews it, from other segments. BRPT's segment note gives both, with the elimination column (Note 46, PDF p. 239).</p><table style="min-width: 175px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Petrochemical and refinery</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Energy and resources</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Property</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Eliminations</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consolidated</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025 external revenue</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,020,007</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>605,187</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,213</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>255</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,630,662</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025 inter-segment revenue</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,505</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>33</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4,538)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024 external revenue</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,785,364</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>596,823</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,546</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>262</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,386,995</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024 inter-segment revenue</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,926</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>210</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5,136)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Inter-segment revenue as a share of revenue before elimination: 2025 = 4,538 / (7,630,662 + 4,538) = 0.06%; 2024 = 5,136 / (2,386,995 + 5,136) = 0.21%</p></blockquote><p>The two large segments report no inter-segment revenue at all. Petrochemical and refinery revenue of 7,020,007 equals CAP's revenue before intragroup eliminations, and energy and resources revenue of 605,187 equals the figure for PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk (BREN), in the material-NCI summaries of Note 34 (PDF pp. 221–222). The only inter-segment revenue comes from the property and other segments, and the note does not say what it is. At BRPT's level, then, the eliminations this page describes are close to zero.</p><p>They happen one level down. Each of the two large segments is a listed sub-group, and its own intragroup trade is eliminated before its figures reach BRPT. BREN's segment note, for example, eliminates 7,205 of revenue from contracts with customers in 2025 (6,519 in 2024), most of it the holding segment's 7,227 of revenue charged to the operating companies, and 6,053 of profit before tax (BREN FS 2025, Note 29, PDF p. 127). BRPT's statements do not show CAP's eliminations between its Indonesian plants, the Singapore business of Aster Chemical and Energy acquired on 1 April 2025 (Note 43, PDF p. 226) and its trading companies.</p><p>Some related-party sales are not eliminated at all. The group, in practice CAP, sold 147,273 to PT Synthetic Rubber Indonesia (SRI) and 45,211 to PT Nusantara Polymer Solutions (NPS) in 2025 (Note 45, PDF p. 236). NPS is a related party in which CAP holds shares, and SRI is a 45 percent associate (Note 14, PDF p. 124), so these sales stay in consolidated revenue, and only the group's share of profit in SRI's unsold year-end inventory is deferred under PSAK 228. BRPT does not disclose that amount, and SRI is carried at nil, so where such a deferral would go is itself a judgement. Related-party sales fell to 2.6 percent of revenue from 9.7 percent (Note 35, PDF p. 222), mostly because revenue more than tripled while these sales fell by 15 percent (195,438 against 231,262).</p><h2>11. Related essays</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest">non-controlling-interest</a>: control, NCI measurement and attribution, which this page assumes.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/inventories-net">inventories-net</a>: cost formulas and the lower of cost and NRV, which set the group cost in sections 3 and 8.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-assets</a>: temporary differences and offsetting rules behind section 7.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments">long-term-investments</a>: the equity method for associates in section 9.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/revenues">revenues</a>: when revenue is recognised, which here waits for the sale outside the group.</p></li><li><p>K1 Business Combinations; K2 Consolidation Worksheet (reciprocal balances, cost versus equity method); K4 Intercompany Plant Assets (the same logic for land and depreciable assets, including BRPT's elimination of a land gain between CAP and SRI); K5 Consolidated Cash Flows; K6 Mutual Holdings.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>B86(c) and B94 require full elimination and attribution but do not say how an upstream elimination is split, so it is an accounting-policy choice. Proportional attribution is common and is used here; the alternative (all to the parent) is not ruled out by the text.</p></li><li><p>The source of BRPT's inter-segment revenue (property and others) and of the eliminations of 29 in segment result and 139,079 in segment assets is not explained in Note 46. That CAP's segment carries large intra-segment eliminations is an inference from its structure (Indonesian plants, a Singapore refinery and trading companies in Note 1), not a disclosed figure.</p></li><li><p>Treating BREN's eliminated revenue of 7,205 as mostly holding-company charges to the operating companies rests on the segment table alone. BREN's tax-line elimination of 998 could be deferred tax on eliminated profit or something else, and is not used here.</p></li><li><p>Measuring the DTA at the buyer's rate follows IAS 12's reversal principle as firms explain it; IAS 12 does not state the rule for this case in so many words. The 22 percent and 19 percent Indonesian rates and the 17 percent Singapore rate are from knowledge; only the 34 percent JOC rate is taken from BRPT (Note 23), and whether CAP qualifies for 19 percent was not checked.</p></li><li><p>The IASB's 2024 equity-method exposure draft may change PSAK 228 para. 28; the IASB decided in October 2025 to do further work and had not concluded by mid-2026. The current text was checked only through secondary sources. Where a downstream deferral goes when the associate (SRI) is carried at nil is a judgement BRPT does not disclose.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): Note 1 (PDF pp. 24, 28; printed 11, 15); Note 3c consolidation policy (PDF pp. 67–68, printed 54–55); Note 4 control over CAP (PDF p. 112, printed 99); Note 14 (PDF p. 124, printed 111); Note 23 (PDF p. 145, printed 132); Note 34 (PDF pp. 221–222, printed 208–209); Note 35 (PDF p. 222, printed 209); Note 41 (PDF p. 225, printed 212); Note 43 (PDF p. 226, printed 213); Note 45 (PDF p. 236, printed 223); Note 46 (PDF p. 239, printed 226). BRPT FS. PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025, Note 29 (PDF pp. 126–127). BRPT FS (subsidiary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 110 <em>Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian</em> (IFRS 10), paras. B86(c) and B94. VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 compiled text, aasb.gov.au, which reproduces IFRS 10). Para. 31: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 212 <em>Pajak Penghasilan</em> (IAS 12): no inventory exception under IFRS and the buyer's-rate measurement, as against the US GAAP inventory exception (ASC 740-10-25-3(e), ASC 810-10-45-8). VERIFIED (web, Deloitte Roadmap: Income Taxes, appendix E.6). Paras. 24 and 74: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 228 <em>Investasi pada Entitas Asosiasi dan Ventura Bersama</em> (IAS 28), paras. 28–29. VERIFIED (web, PKF IAS 28 guide; IFRS Interpretations Committee papers on "unrelated investors"). IASB Exposure Draft ED/2024/7 <em>Equity Method of Accounting</em> and its status (further work decided October 2025, staff papers into 2026): VERIFIED (web, FAR comment letter; IASB Update October 2025; IASB staff paper May 2026), content of the final standard unknown. Upstream attribution as a policy choice: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Disclosure Hub, 2014).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 202 (IAS 2), PSAK 227 para. 10 (IAS 27), PSAK 108 (IFRS 8): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. Indonesian corporate rate of 22 percent and the 3-point reduction for listed companies (UU PPh art. 17 as amended by UU 7/2021), Singapore's 17 percent rate, and Indonesian transfer-pricing rules: NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, <em>Advanced Accounting</em>, ch. 5. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A group earns revenue only when goods leave it. Consolidation therefore removes every intercompany sale and purchase in full, and removes the seller's profit on goods still held by another group company at year end, until those goods are sold to an outsider.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Who bears the deferred profit depends on direction. Profit on a downstream sale (parent to subsidiary) falls wholly on the parent's owners. Profit on an upstream sale (subsidiary to parent) belongs to a subsidiary that outside owners share in, so it is divided between the parent and the non-controlling interest (NCI).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The elimination changes the group's carrying amount of inventory but not its tax base, because Indonesian companies are taxed one by one. The difference is a deductible temporary difference under PSAK 212, so each deferral of profit carries a deferred tax asset at the buyer's tax rate, if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) eliminated inter-segment revenue of 4,538 in 2025 (5,136 in 2024), in USD thousand, to arrive at consolidated revenue of 7,630,662 (BRPT FS 2025, Note 46, PDF p. 239). Its policy states that \"all intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation\" (Note 3c, PDF pp. 67–68).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to eliminate intercompany sales and unrealised profit in a consolidation worksheet, release last year's deferral, attribute both between the parent and NCI, compute the parent's equity-method income, and add the deferred tax.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Why the group cannot profit from selling to itself","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A sale between two group companies moves goods from one pocket of the same owner to another, so from the group's point of view nothing has been sold. The usual justification is that the parent's management controls every intercompany transaction, including authorisation and pricing, and that the group is one reporting entity. A transfer price the parent sets itself cannot be evidence of value.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 110 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) para. B86(c) requires the group to \"eliminate in full intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows\", and adds that profits or losses on intragroup transactions \"recognised in assets, such as inventory and fixed assets, are eliminated in full\". Revenue in the consolidated statements is therefore earned only when a group company sells to an ","type":"text"},{"text":"outside party","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", any entity that is not the parent or one of its subsidiaries.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The NCI essay (","type":"text"},{"text":"non-controlling-interest","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":") covers how control is tested, how NCI is measured at acquisition and how profit, OCI and dividends are attributed to it. This page takes all of that as given and adds one complication. The subsidiary's profit \"as the group sees it\" excludes profit that is still sitting in another group company's warehouse.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Eliminating intercompany sales and purchases","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both group companies record an intercompany sale as if it were with an outsider, so the consolidated totals of sales and cost of sales are each overstated by the transfer price. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"transfer price","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the price one group company charges another.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a parent sells goods that cost it 100,000 to its subsidiary for 120,000, a markup of 20 percent on cost. During the year the subsidiary sells goods that cost it 84,000 (at transfer price) to outside customers for 105,000. The remaining 36,000 is in its warehouse at year end.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Seller's books","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Buyer's books","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase from outsider","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventory 100,000 / Cr Accounts payable 100,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Intercompany sale","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Due from subsidiary 120,000 / Cr Sales 120,000; Dr Cost of sales 100,000 / Cr Inventory 100,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventory 120,000 / Cr Due to parent 120,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sale to outsiders","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Accounts receivable 105,000 / Cr Sales 105,000; Dr Cost of sales 84,000 / Cr Inventory 84,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross profit in the separate books = (120,000 − 100,000) + (105,000 − 84,000) = 20,000 + 21,000 = 41,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group cost of goods sold to outsiders = 84,000 × 100 / 120 = 70,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group gross profit = 105,000 − 70,000 = 35,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The first elimination removes the intercompany sale from both sides.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Sales 120,000 / Cr Cost of sales 120,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The entry is the same whatever happened to the goods afterwards. It changes no profit, only the size of the two lines. Without it, the group would report sales of 225,000 and cost of sales of 184,000, and 120,000 of that revenue, 53 percent, would be sales to itself. An unpaid balance of 120,000 would also be eliminated as a reciprocal balance (K2, entry (6)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Unrealised profit in ending inventory","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Removing the sale leaves a gap of 41,000 − 35,000 = 6,000 between the separate books and the group. That gap is the seller's profit on goods the buyer still holds. Profit on an intercompany sale is ","type":"text"},{"text":"unrealised","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" while the goods remain inside the group, and ","type":"text"},{"text":"realised","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" when a group company sells them to an outsider.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ending inventory at transfer price = 36,000; at group cost = 36,000 × 100 / 120 = 30,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unrealised profit = 36,000 − 30,000 = 6,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cost of sales 6,000 / Cr Inventory 6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After the first entry, consolidated cost of sales is 100,000 + 84,000 − 120,000 = 64,000. The debit brings it to 70,000, the group's cost of the goods sold to outsiders, and the credit brings inventory down to what the group paid outsiders. Consolidated inventory is then 30,000, measured at group cost, as PSAK 202 (formerly PSAK 14, IAS 2) requires for any inventory.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Markup on cost or margin on selling price.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Intercompany pricing is usually quoted one of two ways, and confusing them misstates the elimination.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Markup on cost (20%)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross margin on selling price (20%)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Transfer price of 100 of cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"100 × 1.20 = 120","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"100 / 0.80 = 125","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unrealised profit as a share of the transfer price","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"20 / 120 = 16.67%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"20%","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unrealised profit in 36,000 of ending inventory","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"36,000 × 16.67% = 6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"36,000 × 20% = 7,200","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rule is that the unrealised profit is always computed on the ending inventory ","type":"text"},{"text":"at transfer price","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", using the profit as a fraction of the transfer price. A markup ","type":"text"},{"text":"m","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" on cost converts to a margin of ","type":"text"},{"text":"m","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" / (1 + ","type":"text"},{"text":"m","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), so 20 percent on cost is 16.67 percent of price.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Realising last year's deferral","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit deferred at the end of one year is realised in the next, when the buyer sells the goods. The buyer's opening inventory still carries the transfer price, so its cost of sales this year is overstated by the profit eliminated last year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the subsidiary opened the year with a further 24,000 of goods bought from the parent at the same 20 percent markup, all sold during the year (their cost of sales is left out of section 3's figures).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unrealised profit in opening inventory = 24,000 × 20 / 120 = 4,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"This year's worksheet starts from the separate books, which never saw last year's elimination, so the deferral has to be repeated against opening equity and then released through cost of sales. With the parent on the ","type":"text"},{"text":"equity method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (it carries the investment at cost plus its share of the subsidiary's profit, less dividends; section 6), last year's deferral sits in the parent's investment account, so for a downstream sale the investment is debited.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in subsidiary 4,000 / Cr Cost of sales 4,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The credit lowers cost of sales, so this year's group profit rises by 4,000, the profit earned when the goods finally left the group. The debit restores the investment account, which the parent had reduced by 4,000 last year, so the later elimination of the investment against the subsidiary's equity balances. If the parent carries the subsidiary at cost in its separate statements, as PSAK 227 (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows, the same debit goes to opening retained earnings instead (K2 explains the difference).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Over two years the profit is not lost, only moved into the year the goods were sold outside.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Downstream and upstream sales","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Direction decides who bears the elimination. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"downstream","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" sale runs from parent to subsidiary, so the unrealised profit sits in the parent's own profit and falls wholly on the parent's owners. An ","type":"text"},{"text":"upstream","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" sale runs from subsidiary to parent, so the unrealised profit sits in the subsidiary's profit, which outside shareholders share. The elimination of sales, cost of sales and inventory is the same in both cases. Only the attribution differs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The starting numbers are BRPT's. BRPT owns 34.63 percent of PT Chandra Asri Pacific Tbk (CAP) directly and 3.92 percent through its wholly owned Marigold Resources (Note 1, PDF pp. 24 and 28).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent's effective interest = 34.63% + 3.92% = 38.55%; NCI = 100% − 38.55% = 61.45%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"BRPT consolidates CAP despite holding under half of it because, it judges, the composition of CAP's Board of Directors gives it power over CAP's relevant activities and variable returns (Note 4, PDF p. 112). The NCI essay explains the control test.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"CAP reported revenue of 7,020,007, expenses of 5,572,021 and profit of 1,447,986 for 2025, before intragroup eliminations (Note 34, PDF p. 221). Use that profit as the subsidiary's reported profit, and assume (illustration) that the parent's separate profit before investment income is 200,000 and that the section 2–4 sales run between the two, with opening unrealised profit of 4,000 and closing unrealised profit of 6,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Downstream (parent sells)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream (subsidiary sells)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subsidiary's realised profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,447,986 (unaffected)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,447,986 + 4,000 − 6,000 = 1,445,986","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"61.45% × 1,447,986 = 889,787","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"61.45% × 1,445,986 = 888,558","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent's income from subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"38.55% × 1,447,986 + 4,000 − 6,000 = 556,199","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"38.55% × 1,445,986 = 557,428","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit attributable to owners of the parent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"200,000 + 556,199 = 756,199","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"200,000 + 557,428 = 757,428","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"200,000 + 1,447,986 − 2,000 = 1,645,986","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,645,986","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated profit is the same, because the same 2,000 of net deferral (6,000 − 4,000) leaves the group either way. The split moves. Downstream, the parent's owners absorb all 2,000. Upstream, they absorb 38.55 percent of it, 771, and NCI absorbs 1,229, which is the 757,428 − 756,199 gap.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of profit (upstream) = NCI % × (subsidiary's reported profit + opening upstream unrealised profit − closing upstream unrealised profit)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What PSAK says about the split.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 110 requires full elimination (B86(c)) and attribution of profit to the parent and NCI (B94), but does not say how an upstream elimination is attributed. Two methods are in use: the proportional method, and charging upstream profit wholly to the parent, as for downstream. IFRS 10 leaves the split to accounting policy. Proportional attribution is common and reflects that the profit being deferred is the subsidiary's, and this page and the NCI essay follow it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. The parent's equity method and the worksheet","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under the equity method the parent's own profit already equals the consolidated profit attributable to its owners, which is why it is called a one-line consolidation. The parent therefore has to make the same deferrals in its investment account.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Income from subsidiary = parent % × subsidiary's reported profit + opening unrealised profit released − closing unrealised profit deferred (downstream in full; upstream × parent %)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For the upstream case of section 5, the parent records:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in subsidiary 557,428 / Cr Income from subsidiary 557,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK the equity method is mainly a worksheet device for subsidiaries. In its separate statements a parent may carry a subsidiary at cost, under PSAK 109 or by the equity method (PSAK 227 para. 10).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The consolidation worksheet for the upstream case uses K2's numbering, with the profit eliminations between entries (5) and (6). They could equally come first; the order changes no total. This illustration has no fair value excess, so entries (4) and (5) are empty. Assume (illustration) that the parent's sales are 1,000,000, its cost of sales 700,000 and other expenses 100,000; that CAP's 5,572,021 of expenses splits into 5,000,000 of cost of sales and 572,021 of other expenses; and that the subsidiary paid no dividend.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Debit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Credit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1) Parent's income from subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Income from subsidiary 557,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investment in subsidiary 557,428","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2) NCI share of profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of profit 888,558","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI 888,558","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3) Opening equity of subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"as in K2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"as in K2","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5a) Intercompany sales","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sales 120,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of sales 120,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5b) Opening unrealised profit, upstream","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investment in subsidiary 1,542 (38.55% × 4,000); NCI 2,458 (61.45% × 4,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of sales 4,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5c) Closing unrealised profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of sales 6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inventory 6,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6) Reciprocal balances","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"as in K2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"as in K2","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entries (1), (2) and (5a)–(5c): debits = 557,428 + 888,558 + 120,000 + 1,542 + 2,458 + 6,000 = 1,575,986; credits = 557,428 + 888,558 + 120,000 + 4,000 + 6,000 = 1,575,986","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Income statement","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Eliminations","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sales","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,000,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,020,007","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5a) −120,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,900,007","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of sales","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(700,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5,000,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5a) +120,000, (5b) +4,000, (5c) −6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5,582,000)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other expenses","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(100,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(572,021)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(672,021)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Income from subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"557,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1) −557,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"757,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,447,986","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,645,986","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2) −888,558","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(888,558)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Attributable to owners of the parent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"757,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"757,428","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The check is that the consolidated profit attributable to owners, 757,428, equals the parent's own profit under the equity method. Entry (5b) splits last year's deferral because the sale was upstream. The parent's 38.55 percent goes to the investment account and the NCI's 61.45 percent to opening NCI, since both were reduced when the profit was deferred. For a downstream sale, (5b) would debit the investment with the whole 4,000 and NCI would not appear.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Deferred tax on eliminated profit","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The worksheet changes the group's carrying amount of inventory without changing anyone's tax position. BRPT states that \"the Group is subject to income tax on an entity basis based on profit arising or derived from the tax jurisdiction in which the Group entities are domiciled and operates\" (Note 23, PDF p. 145). Indonesia has no consolidated tax return, so the seller has already paid tax on the 6,000, and the buyer's ","type":"text"},{"text":"tax base","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (the amount the tax authority will let it deduct) is the 36,000 it paid.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 212 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pajak Penghasilan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) compares the consolidated carrying amount with that tax base. A carrying amount below the tax base is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"deductible temporary difference","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", which gives a ","type":"text"},{"text":"deferred tax asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (DTA), tax that will be recovered when the buyer sells the goods and deducts 36,000 against revenue the group sees as costing 30,000. PSAK 110 B86(c) points to it directly, saying the income-tax standard \"applies to temporary differences that arise from the elimination of profits and losses resulting from intragroup transactions.\" Beams treats consolidated income taxes in a later chapter.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), both companies pay the general Indonesian corporate rate of 22 percent (whether CAP meets the listed-company conditions for 19 percent is not checked here).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Temporary difference = tax base 36,000 − carrying amount 30,000 = 6,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DTA = 6,000 × 22% = 1,320","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Deferred tax asset 1,320 / Cr Income tax expense 1,320","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net reduction in group profit after tax = 6,000 − 1,320 = 4,680","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The DTA is measured at the rate that will apply when the difference reverses, which is the buyer's rate, because the buyer holds the inventory and will take the deduction. When the two companies sit in different tax jurisdictions the seller's tax paid and the buyer's DTA no longer offset. CAP's group spans Indonesia and Singapore (Note 1, PDF p. 24). If the buyer were a Singapore company taxed at 17 percent:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DTA = 6,000 × 17% = 1,020; tax paid by the Indonesian seller = 1,320; difference left in tax expense = 300","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rates also differ inside Indonesia, and BRPT's own group shows it. Its geothermal JOC subsidiaries pay 34 percent (Note 23, PDF p. 145), so a DTA on inventory held by such a buyer would be 6,000 × 34% = 2,040. A listed company that meets the public-float conditions pays 3 points less than the general rate (19 percent), so the buyer's own status matters too.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Last year's deferral reverses with the goods. Its DTA of 4,000 × 22% = 880 was set up last year and is released this year, so for an upstream sale entry (5b) becomes:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in subsidiary 1,203 (38.55% × (4,000 − 880)); Dr NCI 1,917 (61.45% × 3,120); Dr Income tax expense 880 / Cr Cost of sales 4,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net effect on this year's group profit = −6,000 + 4,000 + 1,320 − 880 = −1,560","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream, NCI bears 61.45 percent of the after-tax effect and the parent 38.55 percent, so every percentage applied in section 5 is now applied to after-tax amounts. A DTA is recognised only if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it (PSAK 212 para. 24); BRPT, for instance, recognises none on the parent's fiscal losses (Note 41, PDF p. 225). A DTA can be offset only against a deferred tax liability of the same taxable entity, or of another entity that will settle net with the same authority, which Indonesia's entity-by-entity system rules out (PSAK 212 para. 74). That is why BRPT shows deferred tax assets of 122,742 and deferred tax liabilities of 263,652 for the CAP group side by side instead of netting them (Note 41, PDF p. 225).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"US GAAP, which Beams follows, instead defers the seller's tax paid and forbids a buyer's-rate DTA on intra-entity inventory; PSAK has no such exception.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Intercompany losses that signal impairment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An intercompany sale below cost creates an unrealised loss, and the general rule eliminates it in the same way, by writing inventory back up to group cost. PSAK 110 B86(c) adds that \"intragroup losses may indicate an impairment that requires recognition in the consolidated financial statements\". If the loss exists because the goods are now worth less than they cost, the group would have had to write them down anyway under PSAK 202's lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV), so that part of the loss stays.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a parent sells goods that cost it 10,000 to a subsidiary for 8,000. At year end they are still unsold and their NRV is 8,500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss in the parent's books = 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Write-down the group would recognise anyway = 10,000 − 8,500 = 1,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss to eliminate = 8,500 − 8,000 = 500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventory 500 / Cr Cost of sales 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated inventory is 8,500, the lower of group cost of 10,000 and NRV of 8,500. Eliminating the whole 2,000 would carry the goods at 10,000, above what they can be sold for.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Intercompany profit across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Associates and joint ventures.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An ","type":"text"},{"text":"associate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is an entity over which the investor has significant influence but not control, usually through a 20–50 percent holding, accounted for by the equity method. A sale to an associate is a sale to an outside party, because the associate is not in the group, so the revenue stays in full. Only the investor's share of the profit in goods still held is deferred (PSAK 228, formerly PSAK 15, IAS 28, para. 28). Losses that show a fall in NRV or an impairment are recognised in full for downstream sales and to the extent of the investor's share for upstream ones (para. 29).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Associate or joint venture","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sales and cost of sales","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"eliminated in full","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"not eliminated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unrealised profit, downstream","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"eliminated in full, against the parent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"eliminated to the investor's share","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unrealised profit, upstream","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"eliminated in full, split parent / NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"eliminated to the investor's share","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry (illustration: 2,000 profit in goods held by a 45% associate)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Share of profit of associate 900 / Cr Investment in associate 900","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The IASB's 2024 exposure draft on the equity method proposes recognising these gains and losses in full. The IASB was still deliberating in 2026, so this row may change.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Vertically integrated groups.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Petrochemical producers moving olefins into polymer units, plantation groups selling crude palm oil to their own refineries, and coal miners supplying captive power plants all have large, recurring intercompany flows and inventory held at every stage. Segment reporting under PSAK 108 (formerly PSAK 5, IFRS 8) shows them when inter-segment revenue is part of the measure management reviews (para. 23(b)), together with the basis of accounting for transactions between segments (para. 27(a)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investment entities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A parent that qualifies as an ","type":"text"},{"text":"investment entity","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" does not consolidate its investees but measures them at fair value through profit or loss (PSAK 110 para. 31). Sales to those investees are then sales to outsiders and are not eliminated.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Transfer pricing.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Consolidation eliminates the profit whatever the price, so an arm's-length tax adjustment to a transfer price changes current and deferred tax but not consolidated inventory, which stays at group cost.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Note 46","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 108 asks for revenue from external customers and, where management reviews it, from other segments. BRPT's segment note gives both, with the elimination column (Note 46, PDF p. 239).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Petrochemical and refinery","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Energy and resources","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Property","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Eliminations","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 external revenue","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,020,007","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"605,187","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,213","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"255","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,630,662","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 inter-segment revenue","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,505","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"33","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4,538)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 external revenue","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,785,364","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"596,823","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,546","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"262","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,386,995","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 inter-segment revenue","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,926","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"210","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5,136)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inter-segment revenue as a share of revenue before elimination: 2025 = 4,538 / (7,630,662 + 4,538) = 0.06%; 2024 = 5,136 / (2,386,995 + 5,136) = 0.21%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The two large segments report no inter-segment revenue at all. Petrochemical and refinery revenue of 7,020,007 equals CAP's revenue before intragroup eliminations, and energy and resources revenue of 605,187 equals the figure for PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk (BREN), in the material-NCI summaries of Note 34 (PDF pp. 221–222). The only inter-segment revenue comes from the property and other segments, and the note does not say what it is. At BRPT's level, then, the eliminations this page describes are close to zero.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"They happen one level down. Each of the two large segments is a listed sub-group, and its own intragroup trade is eliminated before its figures reach BRPT. BREN's segment note, for example, eliminates 7,205 of revenue from contracts with customers in 2025 (6,519 in 2024), most of it the holding segment's 7,227 of revenue charged to the operating companies, and 6,053 of profit before tax (BREN FS 2025, Note 29, PDF p. 127). BRPT's statements do not show CAP's eliminations between its Indonesian plants, the Singapore business of Aster Chemical and Energy acquired on 1 April 2025 (Note 43, PDF p. 226) and its trading companies.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Some related-party sales are not eliminated at all. The group, in practice CAP, sold 147,273 to PT Synthetic Rubber Indonesia (SRI) and 45,211 to PT Nusantara Polymer Solutions (NPS) in 2025 (Note 45, PDF p. 236). NPS is a related party in which CAP holds shares, and SRI is a 45 percent associate (Note 14, PDF p. 124), so these sales stay in consolidated revenue, and only the group's share of profit in SRI's unsold year-end inventory is deferred under PSAK 228. BRPT does not disclose that amount, and SRI is carried at nil, so where such a deferral would go is itself a judgement. Related-party sales fell to 2.6 percent of revenue from 9.7 percent (Note 35, PDF p. 222), mostly because revenue more than tripled while these sales fell by 15 percent (195,438 against 231,262).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related essays","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"non-controlling-interest","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": control, NCI measurement and attribution, which this page assumes.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"inventories-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": cost formulas and the lower of cost and NRV, which set the group cost in sections 3 and 8.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": temporary differences and offsetting rules behind section 7.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-investments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the equity method for associates in section 9.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"revenues","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": when revenue is recognised, which here waits for the sale outside the group.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"K1 Business Combinations; K2 Consolidation Worksheet (reciprocal balances, cost versus equity method); K4 Intercompany Plant Assets (the same logic for land and depreciable assets, including BRPT's elimination of a land gain between CAP and SRI); K5 Consolidated Cash Flows; K6 Mutual Holdings.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"B86(c) and B94 require full elimination and attribution but do not say how an upstream elimination is split, so it is an accounting-policy choice. Proportional attribution is common and is used here; the alternative (all to the parent) is not ruled out by the text.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The source of BRPT's inter-segment revenue (property and others) and of the eliminations of 29 in segment result and 139,079 in segment assets is not explained in Note 46. That CAP's segment carries large intra-segment eliminations is an inference from its structure (Indonesian plants, a Singapore refinery and trading companies in Note 1), not a disclosed figure.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Treating BREN's eliminated revenue of 7,205 as mostly holding-company charges to the operating companies rests on the segment table alone. BREN's tax-line elimination of 998 could be deferred tax on eliminated profit or something else, and is not used here.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Measuring the DTA at the buyer's rate follows IAS 12's reversal principle as firms explain it; IAS 12 does not state the rule for this case in so many words. The 22 percent and 19 percent Indonesian rates and the 17 percent Singapore rate are from knowledge; only the 34 percent JOC rate is taken from BRPT (Note 23), and whether CAP qualifies for 19 percent was not checked.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The IASB's 2024 equity-method exposure draft may change PSAK 228 para. 28; the IASB decided in October 2025 to do further work and had not concluded by mid-2026. The current text was checked only through secondary sources. Where a downstream deferral goes when the associate (SRI) is carried at nil is a judgement BRPT does not disclose.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): Note 1 (PDF pp. 24, 28; printed 11, 15); Note 3c consolidation policy (PDF pp. 67–68, printed 54–55); Note 4 control over CAP (PDF p. 112, printed 99); Note 14 (PDF p. 124, printed 111); Note 23 (PDF p. 145, printed 132); Note 34 (PDF pp. 221–222, printed 208–209); Note 35 (PDF p. 222, printed 209); Note 41 (PDF p. 225, printed 212); Note 43 (PDF p. 226, printed 213); Note 45 (PDF p. 236, printed 223); Note 46 (PDF p. 239, printed 226). BRPT FS. PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025, Note 29 (PDF pp. 126–127). BRPT FS (subsidiary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 110 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 10), paras. B86(c) and B94. VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 compiled text, aasb.gov.au, which reproduces IFRS 10). Para. 31: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 212 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pajak Penghasilan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 12): no inventory exception under IFRS and the buyer's-rate measurement, as against the US GAAP inventory exception (ASC 740-10-25-3(e), ASC 810-10-45-8). VERIFIED (web, Deloitte Roadmap: Income Taxes, appendix E.6). Paras. 24 and 74: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 228 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Investasi pada Entitas Asosiasi dan Ventura Bersama","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 28), paras. 28–29. VERIFIED (web, PKF IAS 28 guide; IFRS Interpretations Committee papers on \"unrelated investors\"). IASB Exposure Draft ED/2024/7 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Equity Method of Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and its status (further work decided October 2025, staff papers into 2026): VERIFIED (web, FAR comment letter; IASB Update October 2025; IASB staff paper May 2026), content of the final standard unknown. Upstream attribution as a policy choice: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Disclosure Hub, 2014).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 202 (IAS 2), PSAK 227 para. 10 (IAS 27), PSAK 108 (IFRS 8): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. Indonesian corporate rate of 22 percent and the 3-point reduction for listed companies (UU PPh art. 17 as amended by UU 7/2021), Singapore's 17 percent rate, and Indonesian transfer-pricing rules: NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Advanced Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", ch. 5. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"Why a group earns nothing by selling to itself, how the worksheet defers and later releases the profit, who bears it when outside owners are present, and what deferred tax does to it, worked from Barito Pacific's 2025 numbers","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"related_fsli":["inventories-net","non-controlling-interest","deferred-tax-assets","revenues","long-term-investments"],"key_takeaways":["A group earns revenue only when goods leave it. Consolidation therefore removes every intercompany sale and purchase in full, and removes the seller's profit on goods still held by another group company at year end, until those goods are sold to an outsider.","Who bears the deferred profit depends on direction. Profit on a downstream sale (parent to subsidiary) falls wholly on the parent's owners. Profit on an upstream sale (subsidiary to parent) belongs to a subsidiary that outside owners share in, so it is divided between the parent and the non-controlling interest (NCI).","The elimination changes the group's carrying amount of inventory but not its tax base, because Indonesian companies are taxed one by one. The difference is a deductible temporary difference under PSAK 212, so each deferral of profit carries a deferred tax asset at the buyer's tax rate, if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'ff940d50-0c65-48a8-8459-9f0457aa0c40' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
